(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["maitrayaniya"]={"slug":"maitrayaniya","name":"Maitrāyaṇīya","deva":"मैत्रायणीय","note":"Prapāṭhakas I–IV so far","intro":["This document covers <strong>Prapāṭhakas I–IV</strong> of the Maitrāyaṇī Upaniṣad — the frame-narrative (King Bṛhadratha's renunciation and his petition to the sage Śākāyanya), the disclosure of the inner Self and its fivefold prāṇa-embodiment, the doctrine of the <em>bhūtātman</em> (the \"elemental self\" bound by the guṇas), and the <em>pratividhi</em> (remedy) of tapas-sattva-purification crowned by the <em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em> (\"mind alone is saṃsāra\") verse-cluster and the Kautsāyani-stuti. Together these four prapāṭhakas constitute the philosophical core of the Upaniṣad.","<strong>Prapāṭhakas V–VII</strong> — the <em>Kuṭsāyana</em> hymn, the great cosmological-praṇava supplement (Prapāṭhaka VI alone has thirty-eight sections: the two forms of Brahman, the Sāvitrī/Gāyatrī meditation, the <em>śabda-brahman → para-brahman</em> ascent, the sixfold yoga <em>aṅgas</em>, the inner sound, the <em>praṇava</em>-bow, and the <em>amṛta-bindu</em> of the sun), and the closing <em>avidyā</em>-polemic and false-teachers passage — will be appended to <strong>this same file</strong> in a later session, following the multi-session model used for the Bṛhadāraṇyaka."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"A per-verse scholarly edition","title":"Devanāgarī · IAST · pada-by-pada gloss · literal · paraphrase · five-commentary digest","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda · Maitrāyaṇīya śākhā · the Bṛhadratha–Śākāyanya dialogue · No. 24 in the Muktikā list of 108</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"About this installment","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This document covers <strong>Prapāṭhakas I–IV</strong> of the Maitrāyaṇī Upaniṣad — the frame-narrative (King Bṛhadratha's renunciation and his petition to the sage Śākāyanya), the disclosure of the inner Self and its fivefold prāṇa-embodiment, the doctrine of the <em>bhūtātman</em> (the \"elemental self\" bound by the guṇas), and the <em>pratividhi</em> (remedy) of tapas-sattva-purification crowned by the <em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em> (\"mind alone is saṃsāra\") verse-cluster and the Kautsāyani-stuti. Together these four prapāṭhakas constitute the philosophical core of the Upaniṣad.","<strong>Prapāṭhakas V–VII</strong> — the <em>Kuṭsāyana</em> hymn, the great cosmological-praṇava supplement (Prapāṭhaka VI alone has thirty-eight sections: the two forms of Brahman, the Sāvitrī/Gāyatrī meditation, the <em>śabda-brahman → para-brahman</em> ascent, the sixfold yoga <em>aṅgas</em>, the inner sound, the <em>praṇava</em>-bow, and the <em>amṛta-bindu</em> of the sun), and the closing <em>avidyā</em>-polemic and false-teachers passage — will be appended to <strong>this same file</strong> in a later session, following the multi-session model used for the Bṛhadāraṇyaka."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Recension, śākhā, and source","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Maitrāyaṇī (also <em>Maitrāyaṇīya</em>, <em>Maitri</em>, <em>Maitrāyaṇa</em>) Upaniṣad belongs to the <strong>Maitrāyaṇīya / Maitrāyaṇī branch of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong> — the same school that preserves the <em>Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā</em>. It is a comparatively <strong>late</strong> Upaniṣad (post-Buddhist vocabulary, developed Sāṃkhya-Yoga terminology, a quotation-rich \"anyatrāpyuktam / atraite ślokā\" texture), and it is the <strong>only</strong> classical Upaniṣad of the principal/secondary set on which <strong>Śaṅkara wrote no commentary</strong>. Its sole surviving Sanskrit commentary is <strong>Rāmatīrtha's <em>Maitrī-Upaniṣad-Dīpikā</strong></em> (printed by Cowell).","<strong>A note on the source e-text.</strong> The base IAST/Devanāgarī here is mechanically transliterated (via the `indic_transliteration`/sanscript library) from the proof-read ITRANS file `maitri.itx` at <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (transcribed by Sunder Hattangadi). That file's header labels the text \"Sāmaveda,\" and it prepends the <strong>Sāmavedic</strong> śānti <em>āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni</em>. This śākhā-label is a known peculiarity of the e-text; the scholarly consensus (Cowell, Hume, Deussen, Radhakrishnan, van Buitenen) firmly places the Upaniṣad in the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda / Maitrāyaṇīya</strong> school. The text itself, its section-numbering, and the Bṛhadratha frame are reproduced as transmitted; the śānti is given as it stands in the e-text, with this caveat.","This <strong>full (Cowell) recension</strong> numbers the sections more finely than the abbreviated divisions sometimes quoted (e.g. Wikipedia's \"I.4, II.7\"): here <strong>Prapāṭhaka I = 7 sections, II = 11, III = 5, IV = 5</strong> (with IV.4 being a block of fifteen numbered ślokas). The numbering of the transmitted text is followed exactly."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Manuscript & recension variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Reading in this e-text</td><td>Variant / note</td></tr><tr><td>Title</td><td><em>maitrāyaṇyupaniṣat</em></td><td>also <em>Maitrāyaṇīya-</em>, <em>Maitri-</em>, <em>Maitrāyaṇa-Up.</em>; van Buitenen 1962 = the critical edition</td></tr><tr><td>Śākhā label</td><td>\"sāmavedīya\" (e-text)</td><td>corr. <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda, Maitrāyaṇīya śākhā</strong> (scholarly consensus)</td></tr><tr><td>I.7</td><td><em>mahārṇavānāṃ śikhariṇāṃ … dhruvasya pracalanaṃ</em></td><td>e-text carries bracketed marginalia <em>(kimetair vārṇyānāṃ)</em>, <em>(vraścanaṃ vātarajjūnāṃ)</em>, <em>(sthānaṃ vā tarūṇāṃ)</em> — variant readings of the \"cosmic dissolution\" list; Cowell's MSS differ here</td></tr><tr><td>II.4</td><td><em>prāṇo'nīśa</em></td><td>Cowell/van Buitenen <em>prāṇo'nīśātmā</em>; here printed <em>prāṇo 'nīśa ātmā</em> (sandhi run-on in e-text)</td></tr><tr><td>II.5</td><td><em>prajāpatir viśvakṣas</em></td><td>Cowell <em>viśvasṛk</em> / <em>viśvākhyas</em>; \"viśvakṣa\" is the e-text reading</td></tr><tr><td>IV.4</td><td>block of 15 ślokas under one \"‖ 4 ‖\"</td><td>the <em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em> cluster + Kautsāyani-stuti; some MSS distribute these differently</td></tr><tr><td>Frame ordinals</td><td>colophons vary: \"iti prathamaḥ prapāṭhakaḥ\" vs. bare \"… prapāṭhakaḥ\"</td><td>the P4 colophon omits <em>iti</em> and the ordinal in the e-text</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (as transmitted in the e-text)","title":"","sa":["ॐ आप्यायन्तु ममाङ्गानि वाक्प्राणश्चक्षुः श्रोत्रमथो बलमिन्द्रियाणि च ।","सर्वाणि सर्वं ब्रह्मोपनिषदं माहं ब्रह्म निराकुर्यां मा मा ब्रह्म निराकरोत् ।","अनिराकरणमस्त्वनिराकरणं मेऽस्तु तदात्मनि निरते य उपनिषत्सु धर्मास्ते मयि सन्तु ते मयि सन्तु ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni vāk prāṇaś cakṣuḥ śrotram atho balam indriyāṇi ca | sarvāṇi sarvaṃ brahmaupaniṣadaṃ mā'haṃ brahma nirākuryāṃ mā mā brahma nirākarot | anirākaraṇam astv anirākaraṇaṃ me'stu tad ātmani nirate ya upaniṣatsu dharmās te mayi santu te mayi santu | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ‖"],"words":[],"lit":"May my limbs, speech, breath, eye, ear, and also my strength and all my senses grow vigorous. All is the Brahman of the Upaniṣads. May I never deny Brahman; may Brahman never deny me. Let there be no denying — let there be no denying on my part. May the virtues recited in the Upaniṣads dwell in me who am devoted to the Self; may they dwell in me. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>(This is the Sāma-/Chāndogya-Kena śānti; see the source-note above for why it heads a Yajurvedic text in this e-text.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"The five commentaries (and how they are used here)","u":[{"ref":"The five commentaries (and how they are used here)","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Because Śaṅkara left no Maitri-bhāṣya, the Maitrāyaṇī's exegetical stack necessarily differs from the principal-Upaniṣad stacks used elsewhere in this series. The five voices digested at each section are:","<table><tr><td>Siglum</td><td>Authority</td><td>Work / basis</td><td>Standpoint</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rt</strong></td><td><strong>Rāmatīrtha</strong> (16th c.?)</td><td><em>Maitrī-Upaniṣad-Dīpikā</em> — the sole classical Sanskrit commentary, ed. Cowell</td><td>Advaitic, but text-historically alert; supplies the traditional word-division and the <em>adhyāropa-apavāda</em> reading</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cw</strong></td><td><strong>E. B. Cowell</strong> (1870)</td><td><em>The Maitri or Maitrāyaṇīya Upanishad</em>, ed. + transl. + Rāmatīrtha's comm. (Bibliotheca Indica) — the <em>editio princeps</em></td><td>Philological; flags interpolation, recension, and corrupt loci</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hu</strong></td><td><strong>R. E. Hume</strong> (1921)</td><td><em>The Thirteen Principal Upanishads</em></td><td>Lucid English rendering; sober on the text's composite character</td></tr><tr><td><strong>De</strong></td><td><strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (1897)</td><td><em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (Maitrāyaṇa)</td><td>Stresses the Sāṃkhya-Yoga overlay and the <em>bhūtātman/antaḥpuruṣa</em> dualism within a Vedāntic frame</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rk</strong></td><td><strong>S. Radhakrishnan</strong> (1953)</td><td><em>The Principal Upaniṣads</em></td><td>Synthetic; reads Maitri as a bridge between the older Upaniṣads and classical Sāṃkhya-Yoga and theism</td></tr></table>","<strong>Honesty note.</strong> Rt, Cw, Hu, De, Rk all comment on Maitri <em>verse-by-verse or passage-by-passage</em> — so, unlike the minor Yoga-Upaniṣads in this series, the digests below rest on five genuinely Maitri-specific authorities rather than thematic transfer. Where a reading is disputed (notably the <em>bhūtātman</em> dualism and the guṇa-cosmology of IV.5), the divergence is recorded rather than smoothed over."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Maṅgala (opening benediction)","u":[{"ref":"Maṅgala (opening benediction)","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The transmitted text opens with a benedictory dhyāna-śloka and a list-verse of cognate Upaniṣads before the narrative proper begins."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Maṅgala-śloka","title":"","sa":["वैराग्योत्थभक्तियुक्तब्रह्ममात्रप्रबोधतः ।","यत्पदं मुनयो यान्ति तत्त्रैपदमहं महः ॥"],"iast":["vairāgyottha-bhakti-yukta-brahma-mātra-prabodhataḥ | yat padaṃ munayo yānti tat traipadam ahaṃ mahaḥ ‖"],"words":[["vairāgya-uttha","from vi-rāga + ut-sthā","\"arisen from dispassion\""],["bhakti-yukta","instr. cpd","\"joined with devotion\""],["brahma-mātra-prabodhataḥ","abl.","\"through the awakening to Brahman-only\""],["yat padam","n. acc. rel.","\"that station which\""],["munayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","\"the sages\""],["yānti","√yā pres. 3 pl.","\"go (to), reach\""],["tat traipadam","n. acc., \"three-footed\"","\"that three-footed (state)\" — cf. ṚV 10.90.4 tripād"],["ahaṃ mahaḥ","\"I (am) the radiance\"","\"that great Light am I\""]],"lit":"\"Through the awakening to Brahman-alone — (an awakening) joined with the devotion that springs from dispassion — that station to which the sages go: that three-footed Splendour am I.\"","para":"Dispassion (<em>vairāgya</em>) breeds devotion; devotion ripens into the bare realization \"Brahman only is.\" The goal the sages attain is the <em>tripād</em> — the three-quarters-immortal Puruṣa of Ṛgveda 10.90.4 — and the meditator identifies with it: <em>ahaṃ mahaḥ</em>, \"I am that Light.\"","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","takes <em>traipadam</em> as the <em>tripād amṛtam</em> of the Puruṣa-sūkta, anticipating VI.5's sun-Puruṣa"],["E. B. Cowell","marks this as a later prefatory verse, not part of the archaic frame"],["Paul Deussen","read it as the bhakti-inflected vedāntinsation typical of Maitri's final stratum"],["R. E. Hume","renders it plainly as a meditator's self-dedication"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Upaniṣad-list verse","title":"","sa":["मैत्रायणी कौषीतकी बृहज्जाबालतापनी ।","कालाग्निरुद्रमैत्रेयी सुबालक्षुरमन्त्रिका ॥"],"iast":["maitrāyaṇī kauṣītakī bṛhajjābāla-tāpanī | kālāgni-rudra-maitreyī subāla-kṣura-mantrikā ‖"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(The Upaniṣads:) Maitrāyaṇī, Kauṣītakī, Bṛhat, Jābāla, Tāpanī, Kālāgnirudra, Maitreyī, Subāla, Kṣurikā, Mantrikā.\"","para":"A traditional <em>anukramaṇī</em>-style list naming the Maitrāyaṇī alongside its companion Upaniṣads — a scribal/recitational rosary that situates this text within a family of minor Upaniṣads. <strong>Cw</strong> treats it as a late attachment to the manuscript tradition; it carries no doctrinal weight but is preserved here as transmitted.","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I — The Renunciation of King Bṛhadratha","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"The Renunciation of King Bṛhadratha","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The frame-narrative. King Bṛhadratha installs his son, retires to the forest, performs the sun-gazing tapas, and is offered a boon by the sage Śākāyanya. He declines all worldly boons and asks instead for knowledge of the Self, voicing the great lament over the impermanence of the body, of kings, of beings, and of the cosmos itself — the </em>nirveda<em> (world-weariness) that opens the whole Upaniṣad.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"I.1","title":"","sa":["ॐ बृहद्रथो ह वै नाम राजा राज्ये ज्येष्ठं पुत्रं निधापयित्वेदमशाश्वतं मन्यमानः शारीरं वैराग्यमुपेतोऽरण्यं निर्जगाम । स तत्र परमं तप आस्थायादित्यमीक्षमाण ऊर्ध्वबाहुस्तिष्ठति । अन्ते सहस्रस्य मुनिरन्तिकमाजगामाग्निरिवाधूमकस्तेजसा निर्दहन्निवात्मविद्भगवाञ्छाकायन्य उत्तिष्ठोत्तिष्ठ वरं वृणीश्वेति राजानमब्रवीत् । स तस्मै नमस्कृत्योवाच — भगवन्नाहमात्मवित्, त्वं तत्त्वविच्छृणुमो वयम्, स त्वं नो ब्रूहीति । एतद्वृतं पुरस्तादशक्यं, मा पृच्छ प्रश्नम्, ऐक्ष्वाकान्यान्कामान्वृणीश्वेति । शाकायन्यस्य चरणावभिमृश्यमानो राजेमां गाथां जगाद ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ bṛhadratho ha vai nāma rājā rājye jyeṣṭhaṃ putraṃ nidhāpayitvedam aśāśvataṃ manyamānaḥ śārīraṃ vairāgyam upeto 'raṇyaṃ nirjagāma | sa tatra paramaṃ tapa āsthāyādityam īkṣamāṇa ūrdhva-bāhus tiṣṭhati | ante sahasrasya munir antikam ājagāma — agnir ivādhūmakas tejasā nirdahann iva — ātma-vid bhagavāñ chākāyanya \"uttiṣṭhottiṣṭha varaṃ vṛṇīṣva\" iti rājānam abravīt | sa tasmai namaskṛtyovāca — \"bhagavann āham ātma-vit, tvaṃ tattva-vic chṛṇumo vayam, sa tvaṃ no brūhi\" iti | \"etad vṛtaṃ purastād aśakyaṃ, mā pṛccha praśnam, aikṣvākān anyān kāmān vṛṇīṣva\" iti | śākāyanyasya caraṇāv abhimṛśyamāno rājemāṃ gāthāṃ jagāda ‖"],"words":[["bṛhadrathaḥ","m. nom., proper name","\"Bṛhadratha\" (\"of the great chariot\")"],["ha vai","particles","\"verily, indeed\" (narrative past marker)"],["rājye jyeṣṭhaṃ putraṃ nidhāpayitvā","loc. + acc. + abs.","\"having installed his eldest son in the kingdom\""],["idaṃ … śārīraṃ aśāśvataṃ manyamānaḥ","acc. + pres. part.","\"deeming this bodily (frame) impermanent\""],["vairāgyam upetaḥ","acc. + p.p.","\"having attained dispassion\""],["araṇyaṃ nirjagāma","acc. + √gam pf.","\"went forth to the forest\""],["paramaṃ tapa āsthāya","acc. + abs.","\"undertaking the highest austerity\""],["ādityam īkṣamāṇaḥ ūrdhva-bāhuḥ","pres. part. + bahuvr.","\"gazing at the sun, arms upraised\""],["ante sahasrasya","loc.","\"at the end of a thousand (days)\""],["agnir iva adhūmakaḥ","\"smokeless like fire\"","\"like a smokeless flame\""],["tejasā nirdahann iva","instr. + part.","\"as if burning (all) with his radiance\""],["ātma-vit","m. nom.","\"the knower of the Self\""],["varaṃ vṛṇīṣva","acc. + √vṛ impv.","\"choose a boon\""],["āham ātma-vit (na)","nom.","\"I am not a knower of the Self\""],["tattva-vit","m. nom.","\"a knower of Reality (= the sage)\""],["etad vṛtaṃ purastād aśakyam","\"this thing-asked, of old, impossible\"","\"this (Self-question) is, of old, not to be granted (as a boon)\""],["aikṣvākān kāmān","acc. pl.","\"desires fit for an Ikṣvāku (king)\""],["caraṇau abhimṛśyamānaḥ","acc. du. + part.","\"clasping (the sage's) two feet\""],["imāṃ gāthāṃ jagāda","acc. + √gad pf.","\"uttered this verse(-lament)\""]],"lit":"There was a king named Bṛhadratha. Having installed his eldest son in the kingdom, and deeming this body impermanent, he attained dispassion and went forth to the forest. There, undertaking the highest austerity, gazing at the sun with arms upraised, he stood. At the end of a thousand (days) the sage drew near — like a smokeless fire, as if scorching (all) with his splendour — the Self-knowing lord Śākāyanya, and said to the king, \"Rise, rise! Choose a boon.\" Bowing to him the king said: \"Lord, I am no knower of the Self; you are a knower of Reality — so we have heard. Do you, then, tell us (of It).\" \"This thing you ask was of old impossible (to grant as a boon); ask not this question — choose other desires, fit for an Ikṣvāku.\" Clasping the sage's feet, the king uttered this lament.","para":"The classic renunciant opening: a king, having secured the succession and recognized the body's transience, performs extreme solar tapas until a sage of fiery purity appears. When offered any boon, the king refuses kingdoms and pleasures and asks only for Self-knowledge. The sage first deflects — Self-knowledge is <em>aśakyam</em>, not a thing handed over like a gift — and offers worldly boons instead. The king's persistence (clasping the feet) sets up the long <em>nirveda</em>-lament of I.2–7.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>aśakyam</em> as \"not to be conveyed by mere telling, since it is <em>aparokṣa</em> (immediate) realization, not information\" — the sage tests the king's <em>adhikāra</em> (fitness)"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the frame is modelled on the Naciketas/Yama (Kaṭha) and Maitreyī (BĀU) scenes; \"Śākāyanya\" is the teacher, \"Bṛhadratha\" the Ikṣvāku-line king"],["R. E. Hume","observes the <em>agnir iva adhūmakaḥ</em> simile = the ascetic burnt clean of \"smoke\" (the obscuring guṇas)"],["Paul Deussen","sees the refusal-then-yielding as the standard Upaniṣadic gradus of the qualified pupil"],["S. Radhakrishnan","reads the body's <em>aśāśvatatva</em> as the spur of the whole work: the question \"how, given impermanence, is joy possible?\" governs Prapāṭhakas I–IV"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.2","title":"","sa":["भगवन्नस्थिचर्मस्नायुमज्जामांसशुक्रशोणितश्लेष्माश्रुदूषिते विण्मूत्रवातपित्तकफसङ्घाते दुर्गन्धे निःसारेऽस्मिञ्छरीरे किं कामोपभोगैः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["bhagavann asthi-carma-snāyu-majjā-māṃsa-śukra-śoṇita-śleṣmāśru-dūṣite viṇ-mūtra-vāta-pitta-kapha-saṅghāte durgandhe niḥsāre 'smiñ charīre kiṃ kāmopabhogaiḥ ‖"],"words":[["asthi-carma-snāyu-majjā-māṃsa-śukra-śoṇita-śleṣma-aśru-dūṣite","loc. cpd","\"defiled by bone, skin, sinew, marrow, flesh, semen, blood, phlegm, tears\""],["viṣ-mūtra-vāta-pitta-kapha-saṅghāte","loc. cpd","\"a heap of faeces, urine, wind, bile, phlegm\""],["durgandhe niḥsāre","loc. adj.","\"foul-smelling, pithless\""],["asmin śarīre","loc.","\"in this body\""],["kiṃ kāma-upabhogaiḥ","instr. pl.","\"what (use is there) in the enjoyments of desire?\""]],"lit":"\"Lord! In this body — defiled by bone, skin, sinew, marrow, flesh, semen, blood, phlegm, and tears; a conglomerate of dung, urine, wind, bile and phlegm; ill-smelling and without pith — what is the good of the enjoyments of desire?\"","para":"The first stanza of the lament: the <em>kāya-vicaya</em>, an unflinching anatomical inventory of the body's impure constituents (a meditation type later codified in Buddhist <em>paṭikūla-manasikāra</em> and in the Yoga <em>aśuci-bhāvanā</em>). If the vessel of pleasure is this, what worth has pleasure?","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","ties this to <em>vairāgya</em> as the first rung; the catalogue is <em>dehe doṣa-darśanam</em>"],["E. B. Cowell","note the strikingly Buddhist-flavoured \"loathsomeness of the body\" topos, evidence of the text's late date"],["Paul Deussen","compares the body-as-dunghill to BĀU and the Garbha-Up"],["S. Radhakrishnan","reads it as the negative pole whose answer is the <em>amṛta abhayam etad brahma</em> of II.2"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.3","title":"","sa":["भगवन्कामक्रोधलोभभयविषादेर्ष्येष्टवियोगानिष्टसम्प्रयोगक्षुत्पिपासाजरामृत्युरोगशोकाद्यैरभिहतेऽस्मिञ्छरीरे किं कामोपभोगैः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["kāma-krodha-lobha-bhaya-viṣāderṣyeṣṭa-viyogāniṣṭa-samprayoga-kṣut-pipāsā-jarā-mṛtyu-roga-śokādyair abhihate 'smiñ charīre kiṃ kāmopabhogaiḥ ‖"],"words":[["kāma-krodha-lobha-bhaya-viṣāda-īrṣyā","cpd members","\"desire, anger, greed, fear, dejection, envy\""],["iṣṭa-viyoga","\"separation from the liked\"","\"parting from the dear\""],["aniṣṭa-samprayoga","\"union with the disliked\"","\"union with the unwanted\""],["kṣut-pipāsā-jarā-mṛtyu-roga-śoka-ādyaiḥ","instr. pl., \"etc.\"","\"hunger, thirst, old age, death, disease, grief, and the rest\""],["abhihate","loc. p.p.","\"stricken, assailed\""]],"lit":"\"In this body, assailed by desire, anger, greed, fear, despondency, envy, separation from the dear, union with the undesired, hunger, thirst, old age, death, disease, grief, and the like — what is the good of the enjoyments of desire?\"","para":"The second stanza: from the body's <em>substance</em> (I.2) to its <em>afflictions</em>. The list of <em>kleśa</em>-like torments — including the famous pair <em>iṣṭa-viyoga / aniṣṭa-samprayoga</em> (parting from the loved, meeting the loathed) — names the structural sufferings that no pleasure can offset.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","classifies these under <em>ādhyātmika</em> (subjective) suffering"],["Paul Deussen","flag the near-verbatim overlap with the Buddhist duḥkha-formula (the <em>piya-vippayoga / appiya-sampayoga</em> of the first sermon) — again diagnostic of date, though the lament-genre is older (cf. the <em>saṃsāra</em>-weariness of the Mahābhārata)"],["R. E. Hume","keeps the rendering close"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.4","title":"","sa":["सर्वं चेदं क्षयिष्णु पश्यामो यथेमे दंशमशकादयस्तृणवन्नश्यन्त उद्भूतप्रध्वंसिनः ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["sarvaṃ ced idaṃ kṣayiṣṇu paśyāmo yathā ime daṃśa-maśakādayas tṛṇavan naśyanta udbhūta-pradhvaṃsinaḥ ‖"],"words":[["sarvaṃ ca idaṃ","n. nom.","\"and all this (world)\""],["kṣayiṣṇu","n. nom. adj.","\"perishable, given to decay\""],["paśyāmaḥ","√dṛś pres. 1 pl.","\"we see\""],["yathā ime daṃśa-maśaka-ādayaḥ","\"as these gadflies, mosquitoes, etc.\"","\"just as these flies and gnats\""],["tṛṇa-vat naśyantaḥ","adv. + part.","\"perishing like grass\""],["udbhūta-pradhvaṃsinaḥ","m. nom. pl. bahuvr.","\"destroyed (as soon as) arisen\""]],"lit":"\"And we see that all this is perishable — just as these gnats and mosquitoes (and the like), perishing like grass, are destroyed as soon as they arise.\"","para":"From the body to the <em>world</em>: everything is <em>kṣayiṣṇu</em>. The smallest creatures, born and gone in a moment, are emblems of universal transiency (<em>udbhūta-pradhvaṃsin</em> — \"perishing in the very arising\").","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>udbhūta-pradhvaṃsin</em> as <em>kṣaṇa-bhaṅgura</em>, momentariness — and notes (defensively, as an Advaitin) that the verse describes the <em>empirical</em> order, not the Self"],["E. B. Cowell","marks the term's resonance with Buddhist <em>kṣaṇika-vāda</em>"],["S. Radhakrishnan","reads it as the move from personal to cosmic impermanence that the next stanzas universalize"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.5","title":"","sa":["अथ किमेतैर्वा परेऽन्ये महाधनुर्धराश्चक्रवर्तिनः केचित् सुद्युम्नभूरिद्युम्नेन्द्रद्युम्नकुवलयाश्वयौवनाश्ववद्ध्यश्वाश्वपतिः शशबिन्दुर्हरिश्चन्द्रोऽम्बरीषो ननक्तुः सर्यातिर्ययातिरनरण्योऽक्षसेनोत्थमरुत्तभरतप्रभृतयो राजानो मिषतो बन्धुवर्गस्य महतीं श्रियं त्यक्त्वास्माल्लोकादमुं लोकं प्रयान्ति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["atha kim etair vā pare 'nye mahā-dhanur-dharāś cakravartinaḥ kecit — sudyumna-bhūridyumnendradyumna-kuvalayāśva-yauvanāśvavaddhy-aśva-aśvapatiḥ śaśabindur hariścandro 'mbarīṣo nanaktuḥ saryātir yayātir anaraṇyo 'kṣasena-uttha-marutta-bharata-prabhṛtayo rājāno miṣato bandhu-vargasya mahatīṃ śriyaṃ tyaktvāsmāl lokād amuṃ lokaṃ prayānti ‖"],"words":[["atha kim etaiḥ vā","\"now what of these?\"","\"and again — what of these?\""],["pare anye","m. nom. pl.","\"still others, the greatest\""],["mahā-dhanur-dharāḥ","m. nom. pl.","\"wielders of the great bow\""],["cakravartinaḥ","m. nom. pl.","\"universal emperors\""],["sudyumna … bharata-prabhṛtayaḥ","list of royal names","\"Sudyumna, Bhūridyumna, Indradyumna, Kuvalayāśva, Yauvanāśva, Vadhryaśva, Aśvapati, Śaśabindu, Hariścandra, Ambarīṣa, Nanaktu, Saryāti, Yayāti, Anaraṇya, Akṣasena, Uttha, Marutta, Bharata, and the rest\""],["miṣataḥ bandhu-vargasya","gen. abs.","\"while the circle of kinsmen looked on\""],["mahatīṃ śriyaṃ tyaktvā","acc. + abs.","\"having abandoned vast sovereignty\""],["asmāl lokād amuṃ lokaṃ prayānti","abl. + acc. + pres.","\"they pass from this world to that (other) world\""]],"lit":"\"And what (then) of these still greater ones — the great bowmen, the universal emperors: Sudyumna, Bhūridyumna, Indradyumna, Kuvalayāśva, Yauvanāśva, Vadhryaśva, Aśvapati, Śaśabindu, Hariścandra, Ambarīṣa, Nanaktu, Saryāti, Yayāti, Anaraṇya, Akṣasena, Uttha, Marutta, Bharata, and the rest? — Kings who, while their kinsmen looked on, abandoned vast sovereignty and passed from this world to the next.\"","para":"The lament widens to <em>history</em>: the mightiest world-rulers, named in a long roll-call (the <em>cakravartins</em> of legend), all surrendered their splendour and died. If emperors could not keep the world, what is the worth of desire's enjoyments?","coms":[["E. B. Cowell","treats the name-list as a <em>purāṇic</em> catalogue, several names corrupt in the MSS (hence the divergent readings); the point is rhetorical accumulation"],["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>miṣato bandhu-vargasya</em> — \"before their relatives' very eyes\" — as underscoring death's indifference to power"],["S. Radhakrishnan","notes the <em>ubi sunt</em> topos: the great-king roll-call as proof of universal mortality"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.6","title":"","sa":["अथ किमेतैर्वा परेऽन्ये गन्धर्वासुरयक्षराक्षसभूतगणपिशाचोरगग्रहादीनां निरोधनं पश्यामः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["atha kim etair vā pare 'nye — gandharvāsura-yakṣa-rākṣasa-bhūta-gaṇa-piśācoraga-grahādīnāṃ nirodhanaṃ paśyāmaḥ ‖"],"words":[["gandharva-asura-yakṣa-rākṣasa","cpd members","\"Gandharvas, Asuras, Yakṣas, Rākṣasas\""],["bhūta-gaṇa-piśāca-uraga-graha-ādīnāṃ","gen. pl.","\"hosts of spirits, Piśācas, serpents, planet-demons, and the rest\""],["nirodhanaṃ","n. acc.","\"the cessation / destruction\""],["paśyāmaḥ","pres. 1 pl.","\"we behold\""]],"lit":"\"And what of these others still — we behold the destruction of Gandharvas, Asuras, Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, hosts of spirits, Piśācas, serpents, planet-demons, and the rest.\"","para":"From human emperors to the <em>superhuman</em> orders: even the long-lived semi-divine and demonic beings pass away. Mortality is not escaped by climbing the ladder of power or longevity.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","notes that beings of vast lifespan still fall under <em>nirodhana</em> (dissolution), so length of life is no refuge"],["Paul Deussen","read the demonological list as showing the lament's reach across the whole cosmological hierarchy, preparing the cosmic dissolution of I.7"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"I.7","title":"","sa":["अथ किमेतैर्वान्यानां शोषणं महार्णवानां शिखरिणां प्रपतनं ध्रुवस्य प्रचलनं वातरज्जूनां वर्जनं निमज्जनं पृथिव्याः स्थानादपसरणं सुराणाम् । सोऽहमित्येतद्विधेऽस्मिन्संसारे किं कामोपभोगैर्यैरेवाश्रितस्यासकृदिहावर्तनं दृश्यत इत्युद्धर्तुमर्हसीत्यन्धोदपानस्थो भेक इवाहमस्मिन्संसारे भगवंस्त्वं नो गतिस्त्वं नो गतिः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["atha kim etair vānyānāṃ — śoṣaṇaṃ mahārṇavānāṃ, śikhariṇāṃ prapatanaṃ, dhruvasya pracalanaṃ, vāta-rajjūnāṃ varjanaṃ (/ vraścanaṃ), nimajjanaṃ pṛthivyāḥ, sthānād apasaraṇaṃ surāṇām | so 'ham ity etad-vidhe 'smin saṃsāre kiṃ kāmopabhogair yair evāśritasyāsakṛd ihāvartanaṃ dṛśyata ity uddhartum arhasīty andhodapāna-stho bheka ivāham asmin saṃsāre — bhagavaṃs tvaṃ no gatis tvaṃ no gatiḥ ‖"],"words":[["mahā-arṇavānāṃ śoṣaṇaṃ","gen. + n. acc.","\"the drying-up of the great oceans\""],["śikhariṇāṃ prapatanaṃ","gen. + acc.","\"the toppling of the mountain-peaks\""],["dhruvasya pracalanaṃ","gen. + acc.","\"the shifting of the Pole-star\""],["vāta-rajjūnāṃ …","gen. (corrupt; var. vraścanaṃ vāta-rajjūnāṃ \"the snapping of the wind-ropes\")","\"the rupture of the wind-cords (that bind the stars)\""],["nimajjanaṃ pṛthivyāḥ","acc. + gen.","\"the sinking of the earth\""],["sthānād apasaraṇaṃ surāṇām","abl. + acc. + gen.","\"the displacement of the gods from their station\""],["so 'ham … etad-vidhe saṃsāre","\"I, in a saṃsāra of this kind\"","\"I, in such a round of becoming\""],["āśritasya asakṛd iha āvartanaṃ","gen. + adv. + acc.","\"the repeated return here of one who clings (to it)\""],["uddhartum arhasi","inf. + 2 sg.","\"you ought to rescue (me)\""],["andha-udapāna-sthaḥ bheka iva","\"like a frog in a blind well\"","\"like a frog stuck in a waterless well\""],["tvaṃ naḥ gatiḥ","nom. (twice)","\"you are our refuge, you are our refuge\""]],"lit":"\"And what of these others — the drying-up of the great oceans, the toppling of the peaks, the wandering of the Pole-star, the snapping of the wind-cords (that hold the stars), the sinking of the earth, the displacement of the gods from their stations? In a round-of-becoming of this very kind, I (think) 'I am he' — what is the good of the enjoyments of desire, by clinging to which the repeated return to this world is seen? You are able to deliver (me)! Like a frog in a blind well, am I in this saṃsāra. Lord, you are our refuge, you are our refuge!\"","para":"The lament reaches its cosmic ceiling: oceans dry, mountains fall, the seemingly fixed Pole-star drifts, the cosmic \"wind-ropes\" that tether the constellations snap, earth sinks, gods are unseated — <em>pralaya</em> itself. Against this backdrop the ego-thought \"<em>so 'ham</em>\" (here in its bound, mistaken sense, \"I am this perishable one\") condemns the clinger to <em>punarāvṛtti</em>, repeated rebirth. The king's self-image — a frog trapped at the bottom of a dark, dry well — is the Upaniṣad's signature emblem of the soul in saṃsāra; his cry \"<em>tvaṃ no gatiḥ</em>\" (you are our refuge) finally compels the teaching.","coms":[["E. B. Cowell","discusses the textually difficult \"<em>vāta-rajjūnāṃ</em>\" clause and the bracketed variants (<em>vraścanaṃ</em> \"cutting,\" <em>sthānaṃ vā tarūṇāṃ</em> \"the place of trees\"); the imagery is of the <em>dhruva</em>-mooring of the stars by cosmic cords (a notion also in the Viṣṇu-Purāṇa)"],["Rāmatīrtha","reads <em>dhruvasya pracalanaṃ</em> as the dissolution of even what is named \"the Fixed\" — nothing in the manifest is exempt"],["R. E. Hume","renders the frog-in-the-well as the locus classicus of the trapped <em>jīva</em>"],["Paul Deussen","mark this as the structural pivot: the lament's totality (<em>body → afflictions → world → kings → spirits → cosmos</em>) is what makes the question of II.1ff. unavoidable — and the teacher, who deflected in I.1, must now answer"]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I · Prapāṭhaka I — Details","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"Details","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Genre and frame.</strong> Maitri opens not with a cosmogony (like BĀU/Chāndogya) but with a <em>renunciation narrative</em> in the Kaṭha/Naciketas mould: a king, a forest, an extreme tapas, a fiery sage, a refused boon, and a question that cannot be bought. The whole of I.2–7 is a single sustained <em>nirveda</em> (disgust-with-the-world) speech — the longest such passage in the Upaniṣads.","- <strong>The governing question.</strong> Unstated but implied throughout: <em>given that body, world, and cosmos all perish, how is there any abiding good?</em> Prapāṭhaka II answers with the immortal Self; III–IV diagnose why that Self appears bound; V–VI give the yoga that liberates it.","- <strong>Two senses of \"so 'ham.\"</strong> Note the irony: in I.7 <em>so 'ham</em> (\"I am he/this\") is the <strong>bound</strong> ego-identification with the perishable; the same phrase elsewhere in Vedānta is the <strong>liberating</strong> identity with Brahman. The Upaniṣad will move the king from the first sense to the second.","- <strong>Date-markers.</strong> The body-loathsomeness inventory (I.2), the <em>iṣṭa-viyoga/aniṣṭa-samprayoga</em> suffering-formula (I.3), and <em>udbhūta-pradhvaṃsin</em> momentariness (I.4) all show contact with the surrounding śramaṇa milieu — the philological basis for dating Maitri late among the Upaniṣads."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I · Prapāṭhaka I — Visual representation","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"Visual representation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>THE LAMENT OF BṚHADRATHA — widening circles of impermanence (I.2–7)\n\n         ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n         │  I.7  COSMOS — oceans dry · peaks fall ·      │  pralaya\n         │        Pole-star drifts · earth sinks ·       │\n         │        gods unseated                          │\n         │   ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  │\n         │   │ I.6 SUPER-HUMAN — Gandharvas, Yakṣas, │  │\n         │   │      Rākṣasas, serpents, planet-demons│  │\n         │   │  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐  │  │\n         │   │  │ I.5 KINGS — the cakravartins,   │  │  │\n         │   │  │      emperors, all dead         │  │  │\n         │   │  │  ┌───────────────────────────┐  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │ I.3 AFFLICTIONS — kāma,   │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │   krodha, hunger, age,    │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │   death, grief …          │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  ┌─────────────────────┐  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │ I.2 BODY — bone,    │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │  blood, phlegm,     │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │  dung: niḥsāra      │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │   ● &quot;so &#x27;ham&quot; —     │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │   frog in the       │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  │   blind well        │  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  │  └─────────────────────┘  │  │  │  │\n         │   │  │  └───────────────────────────┘  │  │  │\n         │   │  └─────────────────────────────────┘  │  │\n         │   └───────────────────────────────────────┘  │\n         └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                 every ring perishes  →  &quot;tvaṃ no gatiḥ&quot;\n                 (you alone are the refuge)  →  Prapāṭhaka II</pre>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I · Prapāṭhaka I — Practical steps and observations","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"Practical steps and observations","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Anatomical contemplation (I.2).</strong> As a <em>vairāgya</em> exercise, mentally inventory the body's constituents (the classical <em>dvātriṃśad-ākāra</em>, \"thirty-two parts\"). The aim is not self-hatred but the loosening of the reflex identification \"I = this attractive surface.\" Maitri pairs this disgust-pole with the bliss-pole (II.2, <em>amṛtam abhayam</em>) — never let the first stand alone.","2. <strong>The two-column suffering audit (I.3).</strong> Write the structural sufferings in two columns — <em>iṣṭa-viyoga</em> (losses of the dear) and <em>aniṣṭa-samprayoga</em> (impositions of the unwanted) — and observe that no acquisition removes the <em>category</em>; it only changes the contents. This is the seed of the IV.11 teaching \"the mind alone is the cause of bondage and release.\"","3. <strong>The ubi-sunt review (I.5).</strong> Periodically recall that the most powerful figures you can name are gone. The exercise is not morbid but <em>de-absolutizing</em>: it shrinks the apparent permanence of present circumstances.","4. <strong>Name your \"well\" (I.7).</strong> The frog-in-the-blind-well is a diagnostic image: identify the specific dark, dry, walled circuit you keep circling (the <em>asakṛd āvartanam</em>, \"repeated return\"). Naming the well is the first turn toward <em>uddharaṇa</em> (being lifted out).","5. <strong>From lament to refuge.</strong> The structural lesson of Prapāṭhaka I is that <em>nirveda</em> is not the destination but the <strong>engine</strong>: world-weariness, fully felt, converts into the wholehearted \"<em>tvaṃ no gatiḥ</em>\" that alone makes one a fit recipient of the teaching. Despair that does not turn into seeking is incomplete."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka II — The Inner Self and the Fivefold Breath","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka II","title":"The Inner Self and the Fivefold Breath","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Śākāyanya, pleased, begins the teaching. He points to the Self that rises beyond the body; recounts the Vālakhilyas' question to Prajāpati (\"whose is the power that makes this cart-like body conscious?\"); names the inner Person (antaḥpuruṣa) who animates the body with a \"part\" of himself; tells how Prajāpati, finding his creatures lifeless, entered them and split into the five breaths; and closes with the chariot-image and the distinction between the Self as detached witness and as apparent enjoyer.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"II.1","title":"","sa":["अथ भगवाञ्छाकायन्यः सुप्रीतोऽब्रवीद्राजानं — महाराज बृहद्रथेक्ष्वाकुवंशध्वजशीर्षात्मजः कृतकृत्यस्त्वं मरुन्नाम्नो विश्रुतोऽसीत्ययं वाव खल्वात्मा ते कतमो भगवान्वर्ण्य इति तं होवाच ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha bhagavāñ chākāyanyaḥ suprīto 'bravīd rājānaṃ — mahārāja bṛhadratha ikṣvāku-vaṃśa-dhvaja-śīrṣātmajaḥ kṛta-kṛtyas tvaṃ marun-nāmno viśruto 'si iti — ayaṃ vāva khalv ātmā te katamo bhagavān varṇya iti taṃ hovāca ‖"],"words":[["suprītaḥ abravīt","nom. + impf.","\"well-pleased, said\""],["ikṣvāku-vaṃśa-dhvaja-śīrṣa-ātmajaḥ","voc. cpd","\"O scion who art the crest-banner of the Ikṣvāku line\""],["kṛta-kṛtyaḥ","nom.","\"one who has done what is to be done\""],["marun-nāmnaḥ viśrutaḥ asi","gen. + nom.","\"thou art renowned under the name Marut\""],["ayaṃ vāva khalu ātmā te","\"this very Self of thine\"","\"this Self of yours\""],["katamaḥ … varṇyaḥ","interrog. + gerundive","\"which (of the selves) is to be expounded?\""]],"lit":"Then the venerable Śākāyanya, well-pleased, said to the king: \"Great king Bṛhadratha, crest-banner of the Ikṣvāku line, you have accomplished your task; you are renowned by the name Marut. (But) this 'Self' of yours — which one is it that the Lord (= you) would have expounded?\" — thus he said to him.","para":"The teacher accepts the pupil and at once opens the central problem: the word <em>ātman</em> is ambiguous. \"Which self do you mean?\" There is the perishable body-self of Prapāṭhaka I, the <em>bhūtātman</em> of Prapāṭhaka III, and the true inner Self — the rest of the dialogue sorts them.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","takes <em>katamaḥ</em> as the deliberate trigger of the whole inquiry: the disciple must learn there are \"selves\" to be distinguished"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the honorific \"Marut\" and the (textually unusual) reflexive address"],["S. Radhakrishnan","reads II.1 as posing the discrimination — <em>bhūtātman</em> vs. <em>antarātman</em> — that organizes II–IV"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.2","title":"","sa":["य एषो बाह्यावष्टम्भनेनोर्ध्वमुत्क्रान्तो व्यथमानोऽव्यथमानस्तमः प्रणुदत्येष आत्मेत्याह भगवान् । अथ य एष सम्प्रसादोऽस्माच्छरीरात्समुत्थाय परं ज्योतिरुपसम्पद्य स्वेन रूपेणाभिनिष्पद्यत एष आत्मेति होवाच — एतदमृतमभयमेतद्ब्रह्मेति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["ya eṣo bāhyāvaṣṭambhanena ūrdhvam utkrānto vyathamāno 'vyathamānas tamaḥ praṇudaty eṣa ātmety āha bhagavān | atha ya eṣa samprasādo 'smāc charīrāt samutthāya paraṃ jyotir upasampadya svena rūpeṇābhiniṣpadyata eṣa ātmeti hovāca — etad amṛtam abhayam etad brahmeti ‖"],"words":[["bāhya-avaṣṭambhanena","instr.","\"by an external support / prop\""],["ūrdhvam utkrāntaḥ","adv. + p.p.","\"risen upward, departed upward\""],["vyathamānaḥ a-vyathamānaḥ","pres. part. (+ neg.)","\"trembling (yet) untrembling\""],["tamaḥ praṇudati","acc. + pres.","\"drives away the darkness\""],["samprasādaḥ","m. nom.","\"the serene one\" (the self of deep sleep; cf. Chānd. 8.3.4)"],["asmāt śarīrāt samutthāya","abl. + abs.","\"rising up out of this body\""],["paraṃ jyotir upasampadya","acc. + abs.","\"attaining the supreme light\""],["svena rūpeṇa abhiniṣpadyate","instr. + pres.","\"is made manifest in his own form\""],["etad amṛtam abhayam etad brahma","nom.","\"this is the immortal, the fearless; this is Brahman\""]],"lit":"\"That one who, by an external support, has risen upward — trembling yet untrembling — and dispels the darkness: He is the Self,\" said the venerable one. \"And that serene one (<em>samprasāda</em>) who, rising up out of this body and attaining the supreme light, is made manifest in his own form — He is the Self,\" he said; \"this is the immortal, the fearless; this is Brahman.\"","para":"Two pointers to the Self: (1) the principle that \"rises upward\" overcoming inertia and dispels the darkness of ignorance, and (2) the <em>samprasāda</em> — the serene self of deep sleep (a direct citation of Chāndogya 8.3.4 / 8.12.3) — which, leaving the body and reaching the \"supreme light,\" stands forth in its own nature. That, says the teacher, is <em>amṛtam abhayam brahma</em>: the immortal, fearless Brahman.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>bāhyāvaṣṭambhana</em> as the breath/prāṇa that \"props\" the rising; the <em>samprasāda</em> is the <em>suṣupti</em>-self of the Chāndogya, here equated with Brahman"],["E. B. Cowell","flag the explicit Chāndogya borrowing (<em>samprasāda … svena rūpeṇa</em>)"],["Paul Deussen","reads <em>paraṃ jyotiḥ</em> as the \"own light\" of BĀU 4.3.6 (<em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em>)"],["S. Radhakrishnan","sees the verse as Maitri's first positive answer to Prapāṭhaka I's lament: beyond the perishable lies the <em>abhaya</em> (fearless) Self"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.3","title":"","sa":["अथ खल्वियं ब्रह्मविद्या सर्वोपनिषद्विद्या वा राजन्नस्माकं भगवता मैत्रेयेण व्याख्याता — अहं ते कथयिष्यामि — इत्यथापहतपाप्मानस्तिग्मतेजस ऊर्ध्वरेतसो वालखिल्या इति श्रूयन्ते । अथैते प्रजापतिमब्रुवन् — भगवञ्छकटमिवाचेतनमिदं शरीरं कस्यैष खल्वीदृशो महिमातीन्द्रियभूतस्य येनैतद्विधमिदं चेतनवत्प्रतिष्ठापितं प्रचोदयिता अस्य को भगवन्नेतदस्माकं ब्रूहीति तान्होवाच ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha khalv iyaṃ brahma-vidyā sarvopaniṣad-vidyā vā rājann asmākaṃ bhagavatā maitreyeṇa vyākhyātā — ahaṃ te kathayiṣyāmi — ity atha apahata-pāpmānas tigma-tejasa ūrdhva-retaso vālakhilyā iti śrūyante | atha ete prajāpatim abruvan — bhagavañ chakaṭam iva acetanam idaṃ śarīraṃ kasyaiṣa khalv īdṛśo mahimā atīndriya-bhūtasya yenaitad-vidham idaṃ cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ pracodayitā asya ko bhagavann etad asmākaṃ brūhi iti tān hovāca ‖"],"words":[["brahma-vidyā sarva-upaniṣad-vidyā","nom.","\"knowledge of Brahman, the knowledge of all the Upaniṣads\""],["bhagavatā maitreyeṇa vyākhyātā","instr. + p.p.","\"expounded by the venerable Maitreya\""],["apahata-pāpmānaḥ","nom. pl. bahuvr.","\"with evil smitten away, sinless\""],["tigma-tejasaḥ","nom. pl.","\"of sharp/fierce splendour\""],["ūrdhva-retasaḥ","nom. pl.","\"of upward-flowing seed, perfect celibates\""],["vālakhilyāḥ","nom. pl., the thumb-sized sages","\"the Vālakhilyas\""],["śakaṭam iva acetanam","acc.","\"unconscious, like a cart\""],["atīndriya-bhūtasya","gen.","\"of (a being) beyond the senses\""],["mahimā … kasya","nom. + gen.","\"whose is this greatness?\""],["cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ","acc.","\"set up so as to possess consciousness\""],["pracodayitā asya kaḥ","nom.","\"who is its impeller?\""]],"lit":"\"Now this knowledge of Brahman — the knowledge of all the Upaniṣads — O king, was expounded to us by the venerable Maitreya. I shall declare it to you. — There are heard (of) the Vālakhilyas, sinless, of fierce splendour, perfect in continence. They said to Prajāpati: 'Lord, this body is unconscious, like a cart. To what supersensible being belongs this greatness, by which a body of this kind is set up endowed with consciousness, and who is its impeller? This, Lord, declare to us.' To them he said (the following).\"","para":"The teaching is given a pedigree (Maitreya → Śākāyanya) and then framed as a dialogue: the Vālakhilya sages ask Prajāpati the engineer's question — the inert body is a <em>cart</em>; what supersensible power both <em>constitutes</em> it as conscious and <em>drives</em> it? This sets the agenda for II.4–II.9.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","identifies the question as twofold — <em>pratiṣṭhāpitṛ</em> (the constituting ground) and <em>pracodayitṛ</em> (the impelling agent) — answered respectively by the unmoved Self (II.4) and the participating \"part\" (II.5)"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the Vālakhilyas as the diminutive ṛṣis of Ṛgvedic legend, here a frame-device"],["R. E. Hume","render <em>śakaṭam iva</em> \"like a cart\" — the locus classicus of the body-as-vehicle that II.9 develops into the full chariot"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.4","title":"","sa":["यो ह खलु वाचोपरिस्थः श्रूयते स एव वा एष शुद्धः पूतः शून्यः शान्तोऽप्राणोऽनीशः आत्माऽनन्तोऽक्षय्यः स्थिरः शाश्वतोऽजः स्वतन्त्रः स्वे महिम्नि तिष्ठति — अनेनेदं शरीरं चेतनवत्प्रतिष्ठापितं प्रचोदयिता चैषोऽस्येति । ते होचुः — भगवन्कथमनेनेदृशेनानिच्छेनैतद्विधमिदं चेतनवत्प्रतिष्ठापितं प्रचोदयिता चैषोऽस्येति कथमिति तान्होवाच ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["yo ha khalu vāc-opariṣṭhaḥ śrūyate sa eva vā eṣa śuddhaḥ pūtaḥ śūnyaḥ śānto 'prāṇo 'nīśa ātmā 'nanto 'kṣayyaḥ sthiraḥ śāśvato 'jaḥ svatantraḥ sve mahimni tiṣṭhati — anenedaṃ śarīraṃ cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ pracodayitā caiṣo 'syeti | te hocuḥ — bhagavan katham anenedṛśena anicchena etad-vidham idaṃ cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ pracodayitā caiṣo 'syeti katham iti tān hovāca ‖"],"words":[["vāc-opariṣṭhaḥ","nom.","\"standing above speech, beyond utterance\""],["śuddhaḥ pūtaḥ śūnyaḥ śāntaḥ","nom. adjj.","\"pure, cleansed, void, tranquil\""],["a-prāṇaḥ","nom.","\"breathless (free of vital process)\""],["an-īśaḥ","nom.","\"without a ruler over him\" (var. anīśa / Cowell anīśātmā)"],["an-antaḥ a-kṣayyaḥ","nom.","\"endless, indestructible\""],["sthiraḥ śāśvataḥ a-jaḥ","nom.","\"stable, eternal, unborn\""],["sva-tantraḥ","nom.","\"independent, self-dependent\""],["sve mahimni tiṣṭhati","loc. + pres.","\"abides in his own greatness\""],["an-icchena","instr.","\"by (one who is) without desire/will\""]],"lit":"\"He who, verily, is heard of as standing above speech — that very one is pure, cleansed, void, tranquil, breathless, masterless, the Self, endless, indestructible, stable, eternal, unborn, independent, abiding in his own greatness; by him this body is set up as conscious, and he is its impeller.\" They said: \"Lord, how, by such a one — desireless as he is — is a body of this kind set up as conscious, and how is he its impeller?\" To them he said (this).","para":"Prajāpati first answers the <em>pratiṣṭhāpitṛ</em> side: the constituting ground is the Self \"above speech\" — the apophatic Brahman (pure, void, tranquil, <em>aprāṇa</em>, unborn, self-dependent). But this raises the obvious objection that drives the rest of the section: how can a <strong>desireless, actionless</strong> principle constitute and <em>drive</em> a body? The answer (II.5) is the doctrine of the participating \"part.\"","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","reads the string of negations as the <em>nirguṇa</em> description; the seeming contradiction (an actionless mover) is resolved by <em>aṃśa</em> in II.5"],["E. B. Cowell","discusses the variant <em>anīśa / anīśātmā</em> (masterless / the un-lording Self)"],["Paul Deussen","notes <em>śūnyaḥ</em> (\"void\") as a Buddhist-tinged term taken into a Vedāntic apophasis"],["S. Radhakrishnan","stresses <em>sve mahimni tiṣṭhati</em> — the Self rests in its own glory, never acting, yet is the ground of all action"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.5","title":"","sa":["स वा एष सूक्ष्मोऽग्राह्योऽदृश्यः पुरुषसंज्ञको बुद्धिपूर्वमिहैवावर्ततेंऽशेन — सुषुप्तस्येव बुद्धिपूर्वं निबोधयति । अथ यो ह खलु वावैतस्यांशोऽयं यश्चेतनमात्रः प्रतिपुरुषं क्षेत्रज्ञः सङ्कल्पाध्यवसायाभिमानलिङ्गः प्रजापतिर्विश्वः — तेन चेतनेनेदं शरीरं चेतनवत्प्रतिष्ठापितं प्रचोदयिता चैषोऽस्येति । ते होचुः — भगवन्नीदृशस्य कथमंशेन वर्तनमिति तान्होवाच ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa sūkṣmo 'grāhyo 'dṛśyaḥ puruṣa-saṃjñako buddhi-pūrvam ihaivāvartate 'ṃśena — suṣuptasyeva buddhi-pūrvaṃ nibodhayati | atha yo ha khalu vāvaitasyāṃśo 'yaṃ yaś cetana-mātraḥ pratipuruṣaṃ kṣetrajñaḥ saṅkalpādhyavasāyābhimāna-liṅgaḥ prajāpatir viśvaḥ — tena cetanenedaṃ śarīraṃ cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ pracodayitā caiṣo 'syeti | te hocuḥ — bhagavann īdṛśasya katham aṃśena vartanam iti tān hovāca ‖"],"words":[["sūkṣmaḥ a-grāhyaḥ a-dṛśyaḥ","nom.","\"subtle, ungraspable, invisible\""],["puruṣa-saṃjñakaḥ","nom.","\"designated 'Puruṣa'\""],["buddhi-pūrvam … āvartate","adv. + pres.","\"abides/operates here, attended by intellect\""],["aṃśena","instr.","\"by (only) a part\""],["suṣuptasya iva … nibodhayati","gen. + pres.","\"awakens (one) as if from deep sleep\""],["cetana-mātraḥ","nom.","\"pure consciousness, consciousness-only\""],["prati-puruṣaṃ kṣetra-jñaḥ","adv. + nom.","\"the field-knower in each person\""],["saṅkalpa-adhyavasāya-abhimāna-liṅgaḥ","nom. bahuvr.","\"whose marks are resolve, determination, and self-conceit\""],["prajāpatiḥ viśvaḥ","nom.","\"Prajāpati, the All\""],["aṃśena vartanam","instr. + n.","\"operating by a part\""]],"lit":"\"He, verily, this subtle, ungraspable, invisible one, named Puruṣa, comes to abide here — attended by intellect — through a part (of himself); as one awakens a sleeper, (so) he awakens (the body). And that which is, verily, this part of him — which is pure consciousness, the field-knower in each person, whose marks are resolve, determination, and self-conceit, (the cosmic) Prajāpati, the All — by that conscious (part) this body is set up as conscious, and he is its impeller.\" They said: \"Lord, how does such a one operate by a part?\" To them he said (this).","para":"Now the <em>pracodayitṛ</em> side: the actionless Puruṣa \"enters\" embodiment not wholly but <em>by a part</em> (<em>aṃśa</em>) — the <em>kṣetrajña</em>, the consciousness present \"in each person,\" recognizable by its empirical marks (<em>saṅkalpa, adhyavasāya, abhimāna</em> — willing, deciding, ego-claiming). The unmoved Self and the moving \"part\" are one Puruṣa under two aspects. This <em>aṃśa</em> doctrine — the supreme Self present partially as the individual knower — directly anticipates Bhagavad-Gītā 15.7 (<em>mamaivāṃśo jīva-loke</em>).","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","is explicit: the <em>aṃśa</em> is <em>jīva</em>, a reflection (<em>pratibimba</em>) — the one Puruṣa appears as many <em>kṣetrajñas</em> without division, as the one sun in many waters"],["E. B. Cowell","note the technical Sāṃkhya pair <em>kṣetrajña / kṣetra</em> (knower / field)"],["Paul Deussen","highlights the three <em>liṅgas</em> (saṅkalpa-adhyavasāya-abhimāna) as the empirical signature of the embodied self"],["S. Radhakrishnan","ties the <em>aṃśa</em> to Gītā 15.7 and reads II.4–5 as Maitri's solution to the \"actionless mover\" paradox: the Self constitutes (as ground) and impels (as participating part)"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.6","title":"","sa":["प्रजापतिर्वा एषोऽग्रेऽतिष्ठत् । स नारमतैकः । स आत्मानमभिध्यायद् बह्वीः प्रजा असृजत । ता अस्यैवाप्रबुद्धा अप्राणाः स्थाणुरिव तिष्ठमाना अपश्यत् । स नारमत । सोऽमन्यत — एतासां प्रतिबोधनायाभ्यन्तरं प्राविशानीति । अथ स वायुमिवात्मानं कृत्वाभ्यन्तरं प्राविशत् । स एको नाविशत् । स पञ्चधात्मानं प्रविभज्योच्यते — यः प्राणोऽपानः समान उदानो व्यान इति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["prajāpatir vā eṣo 'gre 'tiṣṭhat | sa nāramataikaḥ | sa ātmānam abhidhyāyad bahvīḥ prajā asṛjat | tā asyaivāprabuddhā aprāṇāḥ sthāṇur iva tiṣṭhamānā apaśyat | sa nāramata | so 'manyata — etāsāṃ pratibodhanāyābhyantaraṃ prāviśānīti | atha sa vāyum ivātmānaṃ kṛtvābhyantaraṃ prāviśat | sa eko nāviśat | sa pañcadhātmānaṃ pravibhajyocyate — yaḥ prāṇo 'pānaḥ samāna udāno vyāna iti ‖"],"words":[["agre atiṣṭhat","adv. + impf.","\"existed in the beginning\""],["na aramata ekaḥ","impf. + nom.","\"alone, he took no delight\""],["ātmānam abhidhyāyat","acc. + part.","\"meditating upon himself\""],["bahvīḥ prajāḥ asṛjat","acc. pl. + impf.","\"created many creatures\""],["a-prabuddhāḥ a-prāṇāḥ","nom. pl.","\"unawakened, breathless\""],["sthāṇuḥ iva tiṣṭhamānāḥ","nom. + part.","\"standing like posts\""],["pratibodhanāya abhyantaraṃ prāviśāni","dat. + acc. + subj.","\"let me enter within to awaken them\""],["vāyum iva ātmānaṃ kṛtvā","acc. + abs.","\"making himself wind-like\""],["ekaḥ na āviśat","nom. + impf.","\"being one, he could not (fully) enter/enliven\""],["pañcadhā ātmānaṃ pravibhajya","adv. + abs.","\"dividing himself fivefold\""]],"lit":"\"Prajāpati, verily, existed in the beginning. Alone, he took no delight. Meditating upon himself, he created many creatures. He saw them unawakened, breathless, standing like posts. He took no delight. He thought: 'Let me enter within them, to awaken them.' Then, making himself wind-like, he entered within. Being one, he did not (avail to enliven them). So, dividing himself fivefold, he is called <em>prāṇa, apāna, samāna, udāna, vyāna</em>.\"","para":"A cosmogony in the BĀU 1.4 idiom (the solitary creator who \"takes no delight\" — <em>naivāramata</em>): Prajāpati creates, finds the creatures inert, and enters them as <em>vāyu</em> (breath/wind). One undivided breath cannot animate the whole organism, so he distributes himself as the five vital airs. This grounds the prāṇa-physiology of II.7.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>sa eko nāviśat</em> — \"the single (entry) did not suffice\" — hence the fivefold division; the Self enters as the life-principle"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the close patterning on BĀU 1.4.1–3 (the lonely creator)"],["Paul Deussen","read the fivefold split as the bridge from the metaphysical Self (II.4–5) to the psychophysical organism (II.7–9): the same Puruṣa, now operative as the prāṇas"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.7","title":"","sa":["अथ योऽयमूर्ध्वमुत्क्रामतीत्येष वाव स प्राणः । अथ योऽयमवाञ्चं संक्रामतीत्येष वाव सोऽपानः । अथ योऽयं स्थविष्ठमन्नधातुमपाने स्थापयत्यणिष्ठं चाङ्गेऽङ्गे समं नयतीत्येष वाव स समानः । अथ योऽयं पीताशितमुद्गिरति निगिरतीति चैष वाव स उदानः । अथ येनैताः शिरा अनुव्याप्ता एष वाव स व्यानः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["atha yo 'yam ūrdhvam utkrāmatīty eṣa vāva sa prāṇaḥ | atha yo 'yam avāñcaṃ saṃkrāmatīty eṣa vāva so 'pānaḥ | atha yo 'yaṃ sthaviṣṭham anna-dhātum apāne sthāpayaty aṇiṣṭhaṃ cāṅge 'ṅge samaṃ nayatīty eṣa vāva sa samānaḥ | atha yo 'yaṃ pītāśitam udgirati nigiratīti caiṣa vāva sa udānaḥ | atha yenaitāḥ śirā anuvyāptā eṣa vāva sa vyānaḥ ‖"],"words":[["ūrdhvam utkrāmati","adv. + pres.","\"moves upward\" → prāṇa"],["avāñcaṃ saṃkrāmati","acc. + pres.","\"moves downward\" → apāna"],["sthaviṣṭham anna-dhātum","acc. superl.","\"the grossest portion of food\""],["apāne sthāpayati","loc. + pres.","\"deposits in the apāna (lower region)\""],["aṇiṣṭhaṃ … aṅge 'ṅge samaṃ nayati","acc. superl. + loc.","\"the finest, distributes equally to every limb\" → samāna"],["pītāśitam udgirati nigirati","acc. + pres.","\"what is drunk and eaten, he casts up and swallows down\" → udāna"],["yena etāḥ śirāḥ anuvyāptāḥ","instr. + nom. pl.","\"by which these veins are pervaded\" → vyāna"]],"lit":"\"Now that which moves upward — that, verily, is <em>prāṇa</em>. That which moves downward — that is <em>apāna</em>. That which deposits the grossest portion of food in the apāna-region and distributes the finest equally to every limb — that is <em>samāna</em>. That which casts up and swallows down what is drunk and eaten — that is <em>udāna</em>. And that by which these veins (<em>śirāḥ</em>) are pervaded throughout — that is <em>vyāna</em>.\"","para":"The standard fivefold prāṇa-physiology, with functional definitions: prāṇa (upward/respiration), apāna (downward/excretion), samāna (digestive distribution), udāna (the throat-current of swallowing/regurgitation and, by extension, upward exit), vyāna (the diffusive current pervading the <em>nāḍī</em>-network).","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","matches this to the <em>yogic</em> prāṇa-scheme later used in Prapāṭhaka VI's prāṇāyāma"],["E. B. Cowell","note the definitions are close to Praśna 3 and the medical (Āyurvedic) tradition"],["Paul Deussen","observes Maitri systematizes what older Upaniṣads scatter"],["S. Radhakrishnan","flags that the same Self appears here as physiology — metaphysics and bodily process are continuous"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.8","title":"","sa":["अथोपांशुरन्तर्याम्यभिभवत्यन्तर्याममुपांशुम् — एतयोरन्तराले चौष्ण्यं मासवदौष्ण्यं स पुरुषः । अथ यः पुरुषः सोऽग्निर्वैश्वानरः । अप्यन्यत्राप्युक्तम् — अयमग्निर्वैश्वानरो योऽयमन्तः पुरुषो येनेदमन्नं पच्यते यदिदमद्यते । तस्यैष घोषो भवति यदेतत्कर्णावपिधाय शृणोति । स यदोत्क्रमिष्यन्भवति नैनं घोषं शृणोति ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["athopāṃśur antaryāmy abhibhavaty antaryāmam upāṃśum — etayor antarāle cauṣṇyaṃ māsavad auṣṇyaṃ sa puruṣaḥ | atha yaḥ puruṣaḥ so 'gnir vaiśvānaraḥ | apy anyatrāpy uktam — ayam agnir vaiśvānaro yo 'yam antaḥ puruṣo yenedam annaṃ pacyate yad idam adyate | tasyaiṣa ghoṣo bhavati yad etat karṇāv apidhāya śṛṇoti | sa yadotkramiṣyan bhavati nainaṃ ghoṣaṃ śṛṇoti ‖"],"words":[["upāṃśuḥ, antaryāmī","nom. (two soma-libation cups)","the \"Upāṃśu\" and \"Antaryāmin\" graha-cups (ritual symbols of out-breath/in-breath)"],["etayoḥ antarāle","gen. du. + loc.","\"in the interval between these two\""],["auṣṇyaṃ … māsavat","n. + adv.","\"warmth, as of a (gestation-)month\" (text corrupt; var. māṃsavat \"fleshly\")"],["agniḥ vaiśvānaraḥ","nom.","\"the Vaiśvānara fire (the digestive fire)\""],["antaḥ puruṣaḥ","nom.","\"the inner Person\""],["annaṃ pacyate … adyate","pres. pass.","\"the food is cooked … is eaten\""],["ghoṣaḥ … karṇau apidhāya śṛṇoti","nom. + acc. + abs.","\"a roar one hears on closing the ears\""],["utkramiṣyan … na śṛṇoti","fut. part. + pres.","\"when about to depart (die), one hears it not\""]],"lit":"\"Now the out-breath (Upāṃśu-cup) and the in-breath (Antaryāmin-cup) interpenetrate; in the interval between the two there is heat, a warmth as of (gestation); that is the Person. And that Person is the Vaiśvānara fire. So too it is said elsewhere: 'This Vaiśvānara fire is the inner Person, by whom this food is cooked that is here eaten. Of it there is this sound which one hears on stopping the ears. When one is about to depart, one no longer hears this sound.'\"","para":"Linking the breaths to ritual (the <em>upāṃśu/antaryāmin</em> soma-cups) and to the digestive fire (<em>Vaiśvānara</em>, cf. Chāndogya 5.11–18; Bṛhad. 5.9): the inner Person is the heat of life that \"cooks\" food. The \"sound heard on closing the ears\" — the inner roar of the bodily fire (a phenomenon yogins use as a meditation-object, anticipating the <em>nāda</em> of Prapāṭhaka VI) — ceases as a death-sign when prāṇa is about to leave.","coms":[["E. B. Cowell","treats <em>māsavat / māṃsavat</em> as a textual crux and explains the <em>upāṃśu/antaryāmin</em> as the two soma-graha cups used as breath-symbols"],["Rāmatīrtha","equates the inner heat with <em>jāṭharāgni</em> (the gastric fire) and the inner sound with the life-current"],["Paul Deussen","note the Vaiśvānara borrowing from Chāndogya and the proto-<em>nāda-yoga</em> of the \"sound on closing the ears,\" developed at VI.22"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.9","title":"","sa":["स वा एष पञ्चधात्मानं प्रविभज्य निहितो गुहायां मनोमयः प्राणशरीरो बहुरूपः सत्यसङ्कल्प आत्मेति । स वा एषोऽस्य हृदन्तरे तिष्ठन्नकृतार्थोऽमन्यत — अर्थानशानि — इति तत्स्वानीमानि भित्त्वोदितः पञ्चभी रश्मिभिर्विषयानत्ति । बुद्धीन्द्रियाणि यानीमान्येतान्यस्य रश्मयः, कर्मेन्द्रियाण्यस्य हयाः, शरीरं रथः, मनो नियन्ता, प्रकृतिमयोऽस्य प्रतोदः — येनेरितमिदं शरीरं चक्रमिव परिभ्रमति । मृते च नेदं शरीरं चेतनवत्प्रतिष्ठापितं प्रचोदयिता चैषोऽस्येति ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa pañcadhātmānaṃ pravibhajya nihito guhāyāṃ manomayaḥ prāṇa-śarīro bahu-rūpaḥ satya-saṅkalpa ātmeti | sa vā eṣo 'sya hṛd-antare tiṣṭhann akṛtārtho 'manyata — arthān aśāni — iti tat svānīmāni bhittvā uditaḥ pañcabhī raśmibhir viṣayān atti | buddhīndriyāṇi yānīmāny etāny asya raśmayaḥ, karmendriyāṇy asya hayāḥ, śarīraṃ rathaḥ, mano niyantā, prakṛti-mayo 'sya pratodaḥ — yeneritam idaṃ śarīraṃ cakram iva paribhramati | mṛte ca nedaṃ śarīraṃ cetanavat pratiṣṭhāpitaṃ pracodayitā caiṣo 'syeti ‖"],"words":[["nihitaḥ guhāyāṃ","nom. + loc.","\"hidden/deposited in the cave (of the heart)\""],["mano-mayaḥ prāṇa-śarīraḥ","nom.","\"mind-made, breath-bodied\" (cf. Muṇḍ. 2.2.7)"],["bahu-rūpaḥ satya-saṅkalpaḥ","nom.","\"multiform, of true resolve\""],["hṛd-antare tiṣṭhan a-kṛtārthaḥ","loc. + part.","\"abiding in the heart's interior, unfulfilled\""],["arthān aśāni","acc. + subj.","\"let me enjoy objects\""],["svāni imāni bhittvā uditaḥ","abs. + nom.","\"having burst these (his own apertures), he rose forth\""],["pañcabhiḥ raśmibhiḥ viṣayān atti","instr. + acc. + pres.","\"with five rays he devours objects\""],["buddhi-indriyāṇi … raśmayaḥ","nom.","\"the cognitive senses are his reins/rays\""],["karma-indriyāṇi … hayāḥ","nom.","\"the action-senses are his horses\""],["śarīraṃ rathaḥ, mano niyantā","nom.","\"the body is the chariot, mind the charioteer\""],["prakṛti-mayaḥ pratodaḥ","nom.","\"the whip (goad) is made of prakṛti\""],["cakram iva paribhramati","nom. + pres.","\"whirls round like a wheel\""]],"lit":"\"He, verily, having divided himself fivefold, lies hidden in the cave (of the heart) — mind-made, breath-bodied, multiform, of true resolve, the Self. Abiding in the heart's interior, unfulfilled, he thought, 'Let me enjoy objects.' So, having burst open these his own (apertures), he rose forth and with five rays devours the objects. The cognitive senses are his reins, the action-senses his horses, the body the chariot, the mind the charioteer, and the whip is made of <em>prakṛti</em>. Driven by which, this body whirls round like a wheel. And when (the body) is dead, this body is not set up as conscious, and he is its impeller.\"","para":"The famous <strong>chariot-allegory</strong>, here re-cast from Kaṭha 1.3.3–4: the Self in the heart-cave, <em>manomaya prāṇaśarīra</em> (mind-made, breath-bodied — a phrase from Muṇḍaka 2.2.7), \"bursts\" the sense-apertures to enjoy objects through \"five rays\" (the senses). Body = chariot, action-senses = horses, cognitive senses = reins, mind = charioteer, but Maitri adds its own term: the <strong>whip is made of prakṛti</strong> — i.e. it is <em>nature/the guṇas</em> that goad the whole apparatus into its wheel-like revolving. At death the impeller withdraws and the body is no longer conscious.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","maps the allegory term-by-term to Kaṭha but stresses Maitri's innovation — <em>prakṛti-maya pratoda</em>, the guṇa-whip — which makes the chariot move \"as if of itself,\" preparing the <em>bhūtātman</em> doctrine of Prapāṭhaka III"],["E. B. Cowell","cross-references Kaṭha 1.3.3 and Muṇḍaka 2.2.7 (<em>manomayaḥ prāṇa-śarīra-netā</em>)"],["R. E. Hume","render the <em>raśmi</em> (\"ray/rein\") double-sense"],["S. Radhakrishnan","notes Maitri turns Kaṭha's static diagram into a <em>dynamic</em> one: the wheel \"whirls,\" driven by nature, until knowledge halts it"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.10","title":"","sa":["स वा एष आत्मेत्यदो — वशं नीत इव सितासितैः कर्मफलैरभिभूयमान इव प्रतिशरीरेषु चरति । अव्यक्तत्वात्सूक्ष्मत्वाददृश्यत्वादग्राह्यत्वान्निर्ममत्वाच्चानवस्थोऽकर्ता कर्तेवावस्थितः ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa ātmety ado — vaśaṃ nīta iva sitāsitaiḥ karma-phalair abhibhūyamāna iva pratiśarīreṣu carati | avyaktatvāt sūkṣmatvād adṛśyatvād agrāhyatvān nirmamatvāc cānavastho 'kartā karteva avasthitaḥ ‖"],"words":[["vaśaṃ nītaḥ iva","acc. + p.p. + \"as if\"","\"as if brought under sway\""],["sita-asitaiḥ karma-phalaiḥ","instr. pl.","\"by the white-and-black fruits of action\" (good/bad karma)"],["abhibhūyamānaḥ iva","pres. pass. part. + \"iva\"","\"as if overpowered\""],["prati-śarīreṣu carati","loc. pl. + pres.","\"wanders among the successive bodies\""],["avyaktatvāt … nirmamatvāt","abl. (5×)","\"because of unmanifestness, subtlety, invisibility, ungraspability, and freedom-from-mineness\""],["an-avasthaḥ a-kartā","nom.","\"unfixed, a non-agent\""],["kartā iva avasthitaḥ","nom. + \"iva\"","\"established as if an agent\""]],"lit":"\"He, verily, this Self — as if brought under sway, as if overpowered by the white-and-black fruits of action, wanders among body after body. Yet because of his unmanifestness, subtlety, invisibility, ungraspability, and freedom from 'mineness,' he is unfixed, a non-agent — (only) established <em>as if</em> an agent.\"","para":"The crucial <em>iva</em> (\"as if\"): the Self only <strong>seems</strong> karma-bound and transmigrating. In truth — being unmanifest, subtle, invisible, ungraspable, and without ego-possession (<em>nirmama</em>) — it is <em>akartā</em> (no doer). Its apparent agency and bondage are superimposed. This is Maitri's clearest non-dual moment: the wanderer of II.9 is, seen rightly, the unmoved witness.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","seizes on the repeated <em>iva</em> as the textual proof of <em>adhyāropa-apavāda</em> — agency and bondage are <em>adhyasta</em> (superimposed), not real"],["Paul Deussen","reads it through Sāṃkhya: the puruṣa is <em>akartā</em>, agency belonging to prakṛti; the white/black karma-fruits (<em>śukla-kṛṣṇa karman</em>, cf. Yoga-Sūtra 4.7) only seem to bind"],["S. Radhakrishnan","stress <em>nirmamatva</em> (freedom from mineness) as the ground of the Self's untouchedness"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"II.11","title":"","sa":["स वा एष शुद्धः स्थिरोऽचलश्चालेपोऽव्यग्रो निःस्पृहः प्रेक्षकवदवस्थितः स्वस्थश्च — चरितभुग् गुणमयेन पटेनात्मानमन्तर्धायावस्थित इत्यवस्थित इति ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa śuddhaḥ sthiro 'calaś cālepo 'vyagro niḥspṛhaḥ prekṣaka-vad avasthitaḥ svasthaś ca — carita-bhug guṇa-mayena paṭenātmānam antardhāyāvasthita ity avasthita iti ‖"],"words":[["śuddhaḥ sthiraḥ a-calaḥ","nom.","\"pure, stable, unmoving\""],["a-lepaḥ a-vyagraḥ niḥ-spṛhaḥ","nom.","\"unstained, unagitated, free of longing\""],["prekṣaka-vat avasthitaḥ","adv. + nom.","\"established like a spectator\""],["carita-bhuk","nom.","\"(yet) an enjoyer of (his own) activity\""],["guṇa-mayena paṭena","instr.","\"by a curtain made of the guṇas\""],["ātmānam antardhāya","acc. + abs.","\"having veiled himself\""],["avasthitaḥ … avasthitaḥ","nom. (emphatic repetition)","\"he abides — he (truly) abides\""]],"lit":"\"He, verily, this (Self) is pure, stable, unmoving, unstained, unagitated, free of longing — established like a spectator and self-poised; yet, an enjoyer of (his own) activity, he abides having veiled himself with a curtain made of the guṇas. Thus he abides — he (truly) abides.\"","para":"The closing synthesis of Prapāṭhaka II: the Self is the <em>prekṣaka</em>, the unmoved spectator (the <em>sākṣin</em>) — pure, stable, longing-free. And yet \"as enjoyer\" he draws a <strong>curtain of the guṇas</strong> over himself (<em>guṇa-maya paṭa</em>), thereby appearing as the bound experiencer of III. The same Self is witness (II.11a) and, through the guṇa-veil, the <em>bhūtātman</em> about to be analysed (III). The doubled <em>avasthitaḥ … avasthitaḥ</em> (a colophon-cadence) seals the section: this — the witness behind the veil — is how the Self \"truly abides.\"","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","reads the <em>guṇamaya paṭa</em> as <em>māyā/avidyā</em>: the witness self-veils, and the lifting of that curtain is liberation (the program of IV–VI)"],["Paul Deussen","maps it to the puruṣa–prakṛti relation: the spectator (puruṣa) seems an enjoyer only through the guṇa-screen (prakṛti)"],["S. Radhakrishnan","calls II.11 the pivot of the whole Upaniṣad — <em>sākṣin</em> and <em>bhoktṛ</em> are one Self under and behind the guṇa-curtain"],["R. E. Hume","renders <em>prekṣakavat</em> \"like an onlooker,\" the spectator-self that watches its own play"]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka II · Prapāṭhaka II — Details","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka II","title":"Details","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Architecture of the answer.</strong> The Vālakhilyas' two-part question (II.3) — <em>who constitutes the body as conscious, and who impels it?</em> — is answered in two moves: the <strong>unmoved ground</strong> (the apophatic Self \"above speech,\" II.4) and the <strong>participating part</strong> (the <em>aṃśa</em> / <em>kṣetrajña</em>, II.5). The apparent contradiction of an \"actionless mover\" is Maitri's central puzzle, solved by the <em>aṃśa</em>-doctrine.","- <strong>Three borrowed pillars.</strong> II leans on three older Upaniṣads: the <em>samprasāda</em> (deep-sleep self) of <strong>Chāndogya 8</strong>; the lonely creator who \"took no delight\" of <strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.4</strong>; and the <strong>chariot-allegory of Kaṭha 1.3</strong>. Maitri's signature additions are (a) the <strong>prakṛti-whip</strong> (<em>prakṛti-maya pratoda</em>) and (b) the <strong>guṇa-curtain</strong> (<em>guṇa-maya paṭa</em>) — both introducing the Sāṃkhya guṇas as the operative cause of bondage.","- <strong>The witness/enjoyer hinge (II.10–11).</strong> The repeated <em>iva</em> (\"as if bound,\" \"as if an agent\") and the <em>prekṣaka</em> (spectator) establish that bondage is <em>superimposed</em>. This is the doctrinal seam where Maitri is most explicitly non-dual, and it sets up Prapāṭhaka III's analysis of the bound <em>bhūtātman</em> and IV's remedy.","- <strong>Proto-nāda-yoga (II.8).</strong> \"The sound heard on closing the ears\" — the inner roar of the bodily fire — is named here as a death-prognostic, but it becomes a positive meditation-object (the <em>nāda</em> / <em>anāhata</em> sound) in VI.22. Maitri is one of the earliest Upaniṣadic witnesses to this technique."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka II · Prapāṭhaka II — Visual representation","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka II","title":"Visual representation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>THE ONE PURUṢA UNDER TWO ASPECTS (II.4 – II.11)\n\n      UNMOVED GROUND (II.4)                  PARTICIPATING PART (II.5)\n   &quot;above speech&quot; · śuddha · śūnya            aṃśa · kṣetrajña · cetana-mātra\n   śānta · aja · svatantra · sve mahimni       marks: saṅkalpa · adhyavasāya · abhimāna\n        \\                                        /\n         \\____________ ONE PURUṢA _____________/\n                          │\n              enters as VĀYU, splits 5-fold (II.6)\n                          │\n        ┌─────┬─────────┬────────┬────────┬─────────┐\n      prāṇa  apāna    samāna   udāna    vyāna           (II.7)\n      up     down     digest   throat   pervade\n                          │\n              VAIŚVĀNARA inner fire (II.8) — &quot;sound on closing the ears&quot;\n                          │\n   ════════════ THE CHARIOT OF THE SELF (II.9) ════════════\n        Self (rider, in guhā)\n        mind ......... charioteer (niyantā)\n        cognitive senses .. reins/rays (raśmi)\n        action-senses ..... horses (haya)\n        body .............. chariot (ratha)\n        PRAKṚTI ........... the whip (pratoda)   ← Maitri&#x27;s addition\n              → body &quot;whirls like a wheel&quot; (cakram iva)\n                          │\n   ──────────────── seen rightly (II.10–11) ────────────────\n        akartā · &quot;as if&quot; (iva) agent · nirmama\n        PREKṢAKA — the spectator behind the\n        GUṆA-CURTAIN (guṇa-maya paṭa)  ← the veil lifted in IV–VI</pre>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka II · Prapāṭhaka II — Practical steps and observations","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka II","title":"Practical steps and observations","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Trace the \"as if\" (II.10).</strong> Whenever you catch the thought \"I am bound / I must act / this is happening to me,\" insert Maitri's <em>iva</em>: \"it is <em>as if</em> I were the agent.\" The grammatical particle is a contemplative tool — it pries the witness loose from the superimposed doer.","2. <strong>The spectator-posture (II.11).</strong> Sit as <em>prekṣaka</em>: watch sensation, thought, and emotion as a spectator watches a play, without \"mineness\" (<em>nirmama</em>). Maitri's claim is that this posture is not a feat to achieve but the Self's actual condition, momentarily uncovered.","3. <strong>Locate the five reins (II.9).</strong> In the chariot-diagram, note that the <em>cognitive</em> senses are reins (information) and the <em>action</em> senses are horses (impulse). Practically: steady the reins (attention) before trying to rein the horses (behaviour) — the charioteer (mind) governs both.","4. <strong>Name the whip (II.9).</strong> Identify, in a given compulsion, the <em>prakṛti-maya pratoda</em> — the natural/conditioned goad (a guṇa-driven craving or aversion) that makes the chariot \"whirl like a wheel.\" Seeing the whip as <em>nature's</em>, not <em>yours</em>, is the first slackening of its sting.","5. <strong>The inner sound (II.8).</strong> As a preliminary to the VI.22 <em>nāda</em> practice, close the ears and attend to the bodily roar/hum. Maitri introduces it diagnostically; later it becomes a thread leading inward to the <em>śabda-brahman</em>.","6. <strong>Hold both poles (II.2 with I.2).</strong> Pair the body-disgust of Prapāṭhaka I with the <em>amṛtam abhayam</em> of II.2. The method is never disgust alone, nor bliss alone, but the <em>discrimination</em> between the perishable vehicle and the fearless rider."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka III — The Elemental Self (Bhūtātman)","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka III","title":"The Elemental Self (Bhūtātman)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>If the true Self is the untouched witness (II.10–11), then who is it that is bound, reborn, and tossed by pleasure and pain? Prapāṭhaka III answers: the</em> <strong>bhūtātman</strong> <em>— the \"elemental self,\" the empirical person built of the subtle and gross elements, overpowered by the guṇas of prakṛti. Through the iron-ball-and-fire simile it distinguishes the suffering elemental self from the unscorched inner Person, and catalogues the tāmasa and rājasa traits that bind it.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"III.1","title":"","sa":["ते होचुः — भगवन्यद्येवमस्यात्मनो महिमानं सूचयसि, अन्यो वा परः कोऽयमात्मा सितासितैः कर्मफलैरभिभूयमानः सदसद्योनिमापद्यत इत्यवाचीं वोर्ध्वां वा गतिं द्वन्द्वैरभिभूयमानः परिभ्रमतीति — कतम एष इति तान्होवाच ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["te hocuḥ — bhagavan yady evam asyātmano mahimānaṃ sūcayasi, anyo vā paraḥ ko 'yam ātmā sitāsitaiḥ karma-phalair abhibhūyamānaḥ sad-asad-yonim āpadyata ity avācīṃ vordhvāṃ vā gatiṃ dvandvair abhibhūyamānaḥ paribhramatīti — katama eṣa iti tān hovāca ‖"],"words":[["asya ātmanaḥ mahimānaṃ sūcayasi","gen. + acc. + pres.","\"you indicate the greatness of this Self\""],["anyaḥ vā paraḥ kaḥ ayam ātmā","nom.","\"(then) who is this other, further self?\""],["sita-asitaiḥ karma-phalaiḥ","instr. pl.","\"by the bright-and-dark fruits of action\""],["sad-asad-yonim āpadyate","acc. + pres.","\"enters a good or evil womb\""],["avācīṃ vā ūrdhvāṃ vā gatiṃ","acc. (f.)","\"a downward or upward course\""],["dvandvaiḥ abhibhūyamānaḥ","instr. + pass. part.","\"overpowered by the pairs (of opposites)\""],["paribhramati","pres.","\"wanders round (transmigrates)\""],["katamaḥ eṣaḥ","interrog.","\"which one is this?\""]],"lit":"They said: \"Lord, if you thus point to the greatness of this Self, then who is this <em>other</em>, further self that — overpowered by the bright-and-dark fruits of action — enters a good or evil womb, (takes) a downward or upward course, and, overpowered by the pairs of opposites, wanders round? Which one is this?\" To them he said (this).","para":"The disciples press the obvious objection raised by II.10–11: if the real Self is the unmoved, <em>nirmama</em> witness, then <em>who transmigrates?</em> Who takes good and bad wombs, rises and falls, and is buffeted by the <em>dvandvas</em> (heat/cold, pleasure/pain)? The question forces the introduction of a second, lower \"self.\"","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","marks the turn from <em>paramātman</em> (II) to <em>jīvātman/bhūtātman</em> (III): the question is legitimate, for the empirical sufferer must be accounted for"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the verse recycles the exact phrasing of II.10 (<em>sitāsitaiḥ karma-phalaiḥ … paribhramati</em>) — now asked <em>about</em> a distinct self"],["S. Radhakrishnan","frames III as Maitri's theory of the empirical ego over against the transcendental Self"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"III.2","title":"","sa":["अस्ति खल्वन्योऽपरो भूतात्मा योऽयं सितासितैः कर्मफलैरभिभूयमानः सदसद्योनिमापद्यत इत्यवाचीं वोर्ध्वां गतिं द्वन्द्वैरभिभूयमानः परिभ्रमति । इत्यस्योपव्याख्यानम् — पञ्च तन्मात्राणि भूतशब्देनोच्यन्ते, पञ्च महाभूतानि भूतशब्देनोच्यन्ते, अथ तेषां यः समुदायस्तच्छरीरमित्युक्तम्, अथ यो ह खलु वाव शरीरमित्युक्तं स भूतात्मेत्युक्तम्, अथास्ति तस्यात्मा बिन्दुरिव पुष्कर इति । स वा एषोऽभिभूतः प्राकृतैर्गुणैरित्यतोऽभिभूतत्वात्संमूढत्वं प्रयाति, संमूढत्वादात्मस्थं प्रभुं भगवन्तं कारयितारं नापश्यद् गुणौघैस्तृप्यमानः कलुषीकृतोऽस्थिरश्चञ्चलो लोलुप्यमानः सस्पृहो व्यग्रश्चाभिमानत्वं प्रयात इत्यहं सो ममेदमित्येवं मन्यमानो निबध्नात्यात्मनात्मानं जालेनेव खचरः, कृतस्यानुफलैरभिभूयमानः परिभ्रमतीति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["asti khalv anyo 'paro bhūtātmā yo 'yaṃ sitāsitaiḥ karma-phalair abhibhūyamānaḥ sad-asad-yonim āpadyate … ity asyopavyākhyānam — pañca tanmātrāṇi bhūta-śabdenocyante, pañca mahābhūtāni bhūta-śabdenocyante, atha teṣāṃ yaḥ samudāyas tac charīram ity uktam, atha yo ha khalu vāva śarīram ity uktaṃ sa bhūtātmety uktam, athāsti tasyātmā bindur iva puṣkara iti | sa vā eṣo 'bhibhūtaḥ prākṛtair guṇair ity ato 'bhibhūtatvāt saṃmūḍhatvaṃ prayāti, saṃmūḍhatvād ātma-sthaṃ prabhuṃ bhagavantaṃ kārayitāraṃ nāpaśyad guṇaughais tṛpyamānaḥ kaluṣīkṛto 'sthiraś cañcalo lolupyamānaḥ saspṛho vyagraś cābhimānatvaṃ prayāta ity ahaṃ so mamedam ity evaṃ manyamāno nibadhnāty ātmanātmānaṃ jāleneva khacaraḥ, kṛtasyānuphalair abhibhūyamānaḥ paribhramatīti ‖"],"words":[["anyaḥ aparaḥ bhūtātmā","nom.","\"another, lower 'elemental self'\""],["upavyākhyānam","n. nom.","\"the explanation (of this)\""],["pañca tanmātrāṇi … bhūta-śabdena ucyante","nom. + instr. + pres. pass.","\"the five subtle-elements are called 'bhūta'\""],["pañca mahābhūtāni …","nom.","\"the five gross elements (are also) called 'bhūta'\""],["teṣāṃ yaḥ samudāyaḥ tat śarīram","gen. + nom.","\"their aggregate is called 'the body'\""],["sa bhūtātmā","nom.","\"(what is called body) that is the bhūtātman\""],["tasya ātmā bindur iva puṣkare","gen. + nom.","\"its (higher) Self, like a dewdrop on a lotus-leaf\""],["prākṛtaiḥ guṇaiḥ abhibhūtaḥ","instr. + p.p.","\"overpowered by the guṇas of prakṛti\""],["saṃmūḍhatvaṃ prayāti","acc. + pres.","\"goes to bewilderment\""],["ātma-sthaṃ prabhuṃ … na apaśyat","acc. + impf.","\"saw not the Lord standing within himself\""],["guṇa-aughaiḥ tṛpyamānaḥ","instr. + part.","\"sated/flooded by the surge of the guṇas\""],["kaluṣī-kṛtaḥ a-sthiraḥ cañcalaḥ","nom.","\"defiled, unsteady, wavering\""],["ahaṃ saḥ mama idam","\"I am he; this is mine\"","\"the I-and-mine conceit\""],["nibadhnāti ātmanā ātmānaṃ jālena iva khacaraḥ","pres. + instr.","\"binds himself by himself, as a bird (binds itself) in a net\""],["kṛtasya anu-phalaiḥ abhibhūyamānaḥ","gen. + instr.","\"overpowered by the after-fruits of what he has done\""]],"lit":"\"There is, verily, another, lower 'elemental self' (<em>bhūtātman</em>) who — overpowered by the bright-and-dark fruits of action — enters a good or evil womb … . Here is the explanation of this: the five subtle-elements (<em>tanmātras</em>) are denoted by the word 'bhūta'; the five gross elements (<em>mahābhūtas</em>) are (also) denoted by the word 'bhūta'; their aggregate is called 'the body'; and what was called 'the body,' that is called the <em>bhūtātman</em>; and there is its (higher) Self — like a dewdrop on a lotus-leaf. That (elemental self), verily, is overpowered by the guṇas of <em>prakṛti</em>; and from being overpowered, it goes to bewilderment; through bewilderment it does not see the Lord, the blessed Maker (<em>kārayitṛ</em>), who stands within (itself); flooded by the surge of the guṇas, defiled, unsteady, wavering, craving, longing, distracted, it goes to the state of self-conceit. Thinking thus, 'I am he; this is mine,' it binds itself by itself — as a bird (entangles itself) in a net — and, overpowered by the after-fruits of what it has done, it wanders round.\"","para":"The definition of the <em>bhūtātman</em>: it is the <strong>psychophysical aggregate</strong> — the body built from the five <em>tanmātras</em> (subtle elements) and five <em>mahābhūtas</em> (gross elements), the whole material-elemental person. Within this aggregate dwells the true Self, \"like a dewdrop on a lotus-leaf\" (touching but never wetted — the witness untouched by what it rests on). The <em>bhūtātman</em> is overpowered by the <em>guṇas</em>; bewildered, it fails to see the indwelling Lord (<em>kārayitṛ</em>, the real prompter); and in its conceit it generates the ego-knot \"<em>ahaṃ saḥ, mama idam</em>\" (\"I am that, this is mine\") — by which it nets <em>itself</em>, like a bird tangling in its own snare, and so transmigrates.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","explains the <em>bindur iva puṣkare</em> simile as the relation of <em>sākṣin</em> to <em>upādhi</em>: the Self rests on the elemental self as a water-drop on a lotus-leaf — in contact, never absorbed. He glosses <em>kārayitṛ</em> as the antaryāmin \"prompter\" of II.5"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the technical Sāṃkhya inventory (tanmātra/mahābhūta) and the double sense of <em>bhūta</em> (\"element\"/\"being\")"],["Paul Deussen","stresses the self-binding (<em>ātmanātmānaṃ nibadhnāti</em>) — bondage is reflexive, the ego nets itself"],["S. Radhakrishnan","reads the dewdrop image as the heart of Maitri's anthropology: the elemental self suffers, the Self witnesses, untouched"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"III.3","title":"","sa":["अथान्यत्राप्युक्तम् — यः कर्ता सोऽयं वै भूतात्मा, करणैः कारयिता अन्तःपुरुषः । अथ यथाग्निनायःपिण्डो वाभिभूतः कर्तृभिर्हन्यमानो नानात्वमुपैति, एवं वाव खल्वसौ भूतात्मा अन्तःपुरुषेणाभिभूतो गुणैर्हन्यमानो नानात्वमुपैति । अथ यत्त्रिगुणं चतुरशीतिलक्षयोनिपरिणतं भूतत्रिगुणमेतद्वै नानात्वस्य रूपम् । तानि ह वा इमानि गुणानि पुरुषेणेरितानि चक्रमिव चक्रिणा । अथ यथायःपिण्डे हन्यमाने नाग्निरभिभूयते, एवं नाभिभूयतेऽसौ पुरुषः, अभिभूयतेऽयं भूतात्मा उपसंश्लिष्टत्वादिति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["athānyatrāpy uktam — yaḥ kartā so 'yaṃ vai bhūtātmā, karaṇaiḥ kārayitā antaḥpuruṣaḥ | atha yathāgnināyaḥpiṇḍo vābhibhūtaḥ kartṛbhir hanyamāno nānātvam upaiti, evaṃ vāva khalv asau bhūtātmā antaḥpuruṣeṇābhibhūto guṇair hanyamāno nānātvam upaiti | atha yat tri-guṇaṃ caturaśīti-lakṣa-yoni-pariṇataṃ bhūta-tri-guṇam etad vai nānātvasya rūpam | tāni ha vā imāni guṇāni puruṣeṇeritāni cakram iva cakriṇā | atha yathāyaḥpiṇḍe hanyamāne nāgnir abhibhūyate, evaṃ nābhibhūyate 'sau puruṣaḥ, abhibhūyate 'yaṃ bhūtātmā upasaṃśliṣṭatvād iti ‖"],"words":[["yaḥ kartā saḥ bhūtātmā","nom.","\"the (apparent) agent is the bhūtātman\""],["karaṇaiḥ kārayitā antaḥ-puruṣaḥ","instr. + nom.","\"the inner Person is the (real) prompter, through the instruments\""],["agninā ayaḥ-piṇḍaḥ abhibhūtaḥ","instr. + nom.","\"an iron ball, overpowered by fire (heated)\""],["kartṛbhiḥ hanyamānaḥ","instr. + pass. part.","\"being beaten by the smiths\""],["nānātvam upaiti","acc. + pres.","\"takes on diversity (of shapes)\""],["tri-guṇaṃ caturaśīti-lakṣa-yoni-pariṇatam","acc.","\"the threefold (nature), transformed into 8,400,000 wombs\""],["puruṣeṇa īritāni cakram iva cakriṇā","instr. + nom.","\"set in motion by the Person, as a wheel by the potter\""],["ayaḥ-piṇḍe hanyamāne na agniḥ abhibhūyate","loc. abs.","\"when the iron ball is beaten, the fire is not overpowered\""],["upasaṃśliṣṭatvāt","abl.","\"because of (the bhūtātman's) close conjunction (with the body)\""]],"lit":"\"So too it is said elsewhere: 'The (apparent) agent is, verily, this <em>bhūtātman</em>; the inner Person (<em>antaḥpuruṣa</em>) is the prompter, by means of the instruments. Now, just as an iron ball, mastered by fire and beaten by smiths, takes on diversity (of shape), so indeed this <em>bhūtātman</em>, mastered by the inner Person and beaten by the guṇas, takes on diversity. And the threefold (guṇa-nature), transformed into the eighty-four lakh wombs — this threefold elemental (nature) is the very form of that diversity. These guṇas, verily, are set in motion by the Person, as a wheel by the potter. And just as, when the iron ball is beaten, the fire (in it) is not overpowered, so this Person is not overpowered; (only) this <em>bhūtātman</em> is overpowered, because of its close conjunction (with the body).'\"","para":"The <strong>iron-ball-and-fire</strong> simile, one of Maitri's finest. A smith heats an iron ball (the ball = <em>bhūtātman</em>, the fire = the inner Person) and hammers it into endless shapes (= the 8,400,000 <em>yonis</em>, the standard count of rebirth-species). The hammering produces diversity in the <em>ball</em>, not in the <em>fire</em>: when the ball is struck, the fire is not struck. So the guṇas — driven by the Person \"as a wheel by the potter\" — beat the elemental self into the manifold of births, while the Person remains unbeaten. The elemental self suffers only by <em>upasaṃśliṣṭatva</em>, its intimate conjunction with the body; the Person, though present (as fire in iron), is untouched.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","prizes this as the decisive illustration of <em>saṅga</em> (association) without contamination: fire pervades iron yet is not malleable; the Self pervades the body yet is not the sufferer. The <em>cakriṇā cakram iva</em> (\"as a potter (turns) a wheel\") gives the guṇas a divine impeller"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the <em>84-lakh-yoni</em> figure, otherwise Purāṇic/Jain, here in an Upaniṣad"],["Paul Deussen","reads the simile as a Sāṃkhya statement of <em>saṃyoga</em> (puruṣa–prakṛti conjunction) producing apparent change in prakṛti alone"],["S. Radhakrishnan","calls it the clearest Upaniṣadic image of the untouched Self amid the transformations of nature"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"III.4","title":"","sa":["अथान्यत्राप्युक्तम् — शरीरमिदं मैथुनादेवोद्भूतं संविदपेतं निरय एव मूत्रद्वारेण निष्क्रामत् अस्थिभिश्चितं मांसेनानुलिप्तं चर्मणावबद्धं विण्मूत्रपित्तकफमज्जामेदोवसाभिरन्यैश्च मलैर्बहुभिः परिपूर्णं कोश इवावसन्नम् — इति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["athānyatrāpy uktam — śarīram idaṃ maithunād evodbhūtaṃ saṃvid-apetaṃ niraya eva mūtra-dvāreṇa niṣkrāmat asthibhiś citaṃ māṃsenānuliptaṃ carmaṇāvabaddhaṃ viṇ-mūtra-pitta-kapha-majjā-medo-vasābhir anyaiś ca malair bahubhiḥ paripūrṇaṃ kośa ivāvasannam — iti ‖"],"words":[["maithunāt eva udbhūtaṃ","abl. + p.p.","\"sprung from copulation\""],["saṃvid-apetaṃ","n.","\"devoid of consciousness (in itself)\""],["niraya eva","nom.","\"(a) very hell\""],["mūtra-dvāreṇa niṣkrāmat","instr. + part.","\"issuing forth through the urinary passage\""],["asthibhiḥ citaṃ","instr. + p.p.","\"piled up with bones\""],["māṃsena anuliptaṃ","instr. + p.p.","\"plastered over with flesh\""],["carmaṇā avabaddhaṃ","instr. + p.p.","\"bound with skin\""],["viṣ-mūtra-pitta-kapha-majjā-medas-vasābhiḥ","instr. pl.","\"with faeces, urine, bile, phlegm, marrow, fat, grease\""],["malaiḥ bahubhiḥ paripūrṇaṃ","instr. + p.p.","\"filled with many impurities\""],["kośaḥ iva avasannam","nom. + p.p.","\"ruined, like a (dilapidated) treasure-chest\""]],"lit":"\"So too it is said elsewhere: 'This body, sprung from copulation, devoid (in itself) of consciousness, a very hell, issuing through the urinary passage, piled up with bones, plastered with flesh, bound round with skin, crammed with faeces, urine, bile, phlegm, marrow, fat, grease, and many other impurities — (is) like a treasure-chest fallen to ruin.'\"","para":"A second body-loathsomeness passage (cf. I.2), now placed within the <em>bhūtātman</em> analysis: the body is <em>saṃvid-apeta</em> — without consciousness of its own (it is conscious only by the borrowed light of the Self) — generated, impure, and decaying. The point is not mere revulsion but the radical separation of the conscious witness from its unconscious, perishable vehicle.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","notes the body is <em>saṃvid-apeta</em> \"in itself,\" consciousness being the Self's; the disgust serves <em>viveka</em> (discrimination), not nihilism"],["E. B. Cowell","again flag the topos' śramaṇa colouring"],["S. Radhakrishnan","observes the verse closes the loop with I.2 — the lament's body returns, now theorized as the <em>bhūtātman</em>'s shell"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"III.5","title":"","sa":["अथान्यत्राप्युक्तम् — संमोहो भयं विषादो निद्रा तन्द्री प्रमादो जरा शोकः क्षुत्पिपासा कार्पण्यं क्रोधो नास्तिक्यमज्ञानं मात्सर्यं नैष्कारुण्यं मूढत्वं निर्व्रीडत्वं निकृतत्वमुद्धतत्वमसमत्वमिति तामसानि । तृष्णा स्नेहो रागो लोभो हिंसा रतिर्दृष्टिर्व्यापृतत्वमीर्ष्या कामोऽवस्थितत्वं चञ्चलत्वं जिहीर्षा अर्थोपार्जनं मित्रानुग्रहणं परिग्रहावलम्बोऽनिष्टेष्विन्द्रियार्थेषु द्वेष इष्टेष्वभिष्वङ्ग इति राजसानि — एतैरभिभूत इत्ययं भूतात्मा तस्मान्नानारूपाण्याप्नोतीत्याप्नोतीति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["athānyatrāpy uktam — saṃmoho bhayaṃ viṣādo nidrā tandrī pramādo jarā śokaḥ kṣut-pipāsā kārpaṇyaṃ krodho nāstikyam ajñānaṃ mātsaryaṃ naiṣkāruṇyaṃ mūḍhatvaṃ nirvrīḍatvaṃ nikṛtatvam uddhatatvam asamatvam iti tāmasāni | tṛṣṇā sneho rāgo lobho hiṃsā ratir dṛṣṭir vyāpṛtatvam īrṣyā kāmo 'vasthitatvaṃ cañcalatvaṃ jihīrṣā arthopārjanaṃ mitrānugrahaṇaṃ parigrahāvalambo 'niṣṭeṣv indriyārtheṣu dveṣa iṣṭeṣv abhiṣvaṅga iti rājasāni — etair abhibhūta ity ayaṃ bhūtātmā tasmān nānā-rūpāṇy āpnotīty āpnotīti ‖"],"words":[["saṃmohaḥ bhayaṃ viṣādaḥ","nom.","\"delusion, fear, despondency\""],["nidrā tandrī pramādaḥ","nom.","\"sleep, sloth, heedlessness\""],["jarā śokaḥ kṣut-pipāsā","nom.","\"decay, grief, hunger-and-thirst\""],["kārpaṇyaṃ krodhaḥ nāstikyam","nom.","\"niggardliness, anger, denial-of-(the-sacred)\""],["ajñānaṃ mātsaryaṃ naiṣkāruṇyaṃ","nom.","\"ignorance, envy, pitilessness\""],["mūḍhatvaṃ nirvrīḍatvaṃ nikṛtatvaṃ","nom.","\"stupidity, shamelessness, baseness\""],["uddhatatvaṃ asamatvaṃ","nom.","\"arrogance, inconstancy\" → tāmasāni (marks of tamas)"],["tṛṣṇā snehaḥ rāgaḥ lobhaḥ","nom.","\"craving, fondness, passion, greed\""],["hiṃsā ratiḥ dṛṣṭiḥ vyāpṛtatvaṃ","nom.","\"violence, sense-delight, (display of) opinion, busyness\""],["īrṣyā kāmaḥ avasthitatvaṃ cañcalatvaṃ","nom.","\"envy, lust, (false) fixity, fickleness\""],["jihīrṣā artha-upārjanaṃ","nom.","\"wish-to-grab, wealth-getting\""],["mitra-anugrahaṇaṃ parigraha-avalambaḥ","nom.","\"favouring of friends, dependence on possessions\""],["aniṣṭeṣu … dveṣaḥ, iṣṭeṣu abhiṣvaṅgaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"hatred toward the unwanted, clinging toward the wanted\" → rājasāni"],["etaiḥ abhibhūtaḥ … nānā-rūpāṇi āpnoti","instr. + acc. + pres.","\"overpowered by these, he attains manifold forms\""]],"lit":"\"So too it is said elsewhere: 'Delusion, fear, despondency, sleep, sloth, heedlessness, decay, grief, hunger and thirst, niggardliness, anger, denial (of the sacred), ignorance, envy, pitilessness, stupidity, shamelessness, baseness, arrogance, inconstancy' — these are the marks of <em>tamas</em>. 'Craving, fondness, passion, greed, violence, sense-delight, opinionatedness, busyness, envy, lust, false fixity, fickleness, the wish to grab, wealth-getting, partiality to friends, reliance on possessions, hatred toward unwanted sense-objects, and clinging toward the wanted' — these are the marks of <em>rajas</em>. Overpowered by these, this <em>bhūtātman</em> therefore attains manifold forms — yea, attains them.\"","para":"A diagnostic catalogue of the <strong>tāmasa</strong> and <strong>rājasa</strong> traits that \"overpower\" the elemental self (the <em>sāttvika</em> set is treated in the cosmology of IV.5 / VI). These are not random vices but a guṇa-typology of the bound personality: tamas as the cluster of inertia, dullness, and despondency; rajas as the cluster of craving, agitation, and the love–hate axis (<em>dveṣa</em> toward the unwanted, <em>abhiṣvaṅga</em> toward the wanted). It is <em>these</em> — the guṇa-affects — that drive the <em>bhūtātman</em> into \"manifold forms,\" i.e. repeated and varied births. The emphatic repetition (<em>āpnoti ity āpnoti</em>) closes Prapāṭhaka III's diagnosis: thus bound, it transmigrates.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","sorts the lists strictly by guṇa and notes that the climactic pair <em>dveṣa/abhiṣvaṅga</em> (aversion/clinging) is the engine of rebirth — the same pair Patañjali names <em>dveṣa</em> and <em>rāga</em> among the <em>kleśas</em> (YS 2.3)"],["E. B. Cowell","observes the catalogue's overlap with the Gītā's guṇa-portraits (BhG 14, 18)"],["Paul Deussen","reads it as Maitri importing the developed guṇa-psychology of classical Sāṃkhya"],["S. Radhakrishnan","notes that the diagnosis (III) demands the therapy (IV): having shown <em>what</em> binds the elemental self, the text must now give the <em>pratividhi</em>, the remedy"]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka III · Prapāṭhaka III — Details","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka III","title":"Details","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Two selves, one Self.</strong> Maitri's solution to the \"who transmigrates?\" problem (III.1) is to posit the <em>bhūtātman</em> — the empirical, elemental person — as the bearer of bondage, while the <em>paramātman</em> of Prapāṭhaka II remains the untouched witness. The dewdrop-on-lotus-leaf (III.2) and iron-ball-and-fire (III.3) similes both encode the <strong>contact-without-contamination</strong> relation between them.","- <strong>The reflexive knot.</strong> Bondage is <em>self</em>-inflicted: the <em>bhūtātman</em> \"binds itself by itself\" (<em>ātmanātmānaṃ nibadhnāti</em>) with the ego-formula \"<em>ahaṃ saḥ, mama idam</em>\" — like a bird tangling in its own net (III.2). This reflexivity is why the remedy (IV) is the <em>purification of the mind</em>, not an external rescue.","- <strong>Sāṃkhya scaffolding.</strong> The technical apparatus — <em>tanmātra / mahābhūta</em>, <em>kṣetrajña</em>, <em>triguṇa</em>, <em>84-lakh yonis</em>, the guṇa-typology of traits (III.5) — shows Maitri working with a developed Sāṃkhya vocabulary, but housed within a Vedāntic frame (the indwelling <em>prabhu/kārayitṛ</em> whom the bewildered self fails to see).","- <strong>The unseen Lord (III.2).</strong> A striking theistic note: bewildered by the guṇas, the elemental self \"does not see the Lord, the blessed Maker, standing within itself.\" The remedy will be precisely to <em>see</em> this indweller — anticipating the theism of IV.5–6 and the Kautsāyani-stuti (IV.4)."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka III · Prapāṭhaka III — Visual representation","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka III","title":"Visual representation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>THE BHŪTĀTMAN — contact without contamination\n\n   tanmātras (5)  +  mahābhūtas (5)\n          └────────┬────────┘\n                AGGREGATE = &quot;the body&quot; = BHŪTĀTMAN (III.2)\n                     │   ····· dewdrop on lotus-leaf ·····\n                     │        ●  ātmā (the Self, resting, never wetted)\n                     │\n       overpowered by GUṆAS of prakṛti\n                     │\n              saṃmūḍhatva (bewilderment)\n                     │\n        fails to see  ►  PRABHU / KĀRAYITṚ within (the unseen Lord)\n                     │\n        ego-knot:  &quot;ahaṃ saḥ · mama idam&quot;\n                     │\n        binds itself by itself  ──►  like a bird in its own net\n                     │\n                 NĀNĀTVA — the manifold of births (84 lakh yonis)\n\n\n   IRON-BALL &amp; FIRE (III.3)              guṇa-typology (III.5)\n   ┌───────────────────────┐     TAMAS: delusion·fear·sleep·sloth·\n   │  fire  = antaḥpuruṣa   │            decay·grief·anger·ignorance·\n   │  (pervades, UNbeaten)  │            envy·pride·inconstancy …\n   │  iron  = bhūtātman     │     RAJAS: craving·passion·greed·violence·\n   │  hammer= guṇas         │            lust·busyness·wealth-getting·\n   │  potter= puruṣa turns  │            DVEṢA (to the unwanted) +\n   │          the wheel     │            ABHIṢVAṄGA (to the wanted)\n   │  → ball takes shapes,  │            └─ the rebirth-engine ─┘\n   │    fire stays fire     │\n   └───────────────────────┘</pre>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka III · Prapāṭhaka III — Practical steps and observations","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka III","title":"Practical steps and observations","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>The dewdrop test (III.2).</strong> When suffering arises, ask: <em>is this happening to the lotus-leaf or to the dew?</em> The body-mind aggregate (leaf) bears the weight; the witness (dew) rests on it untouched. The image trains the discrimination that the affliction belongs to the <em>bhūtātman</em>, not to you-as-witness.","2. <strong>Find the unseen Lord (III.2).</strong> The bewildered self \"does not see the <em>kārayitṛ</em> within.\" The corrective is to look, in any moment of compulsion, for the still prompter behind the prompted — the awareness in which the guṇa-storm is seen. Maitri makes <em>not-seeing</em> the indweller the very definition of bondage.","3. <strong>Untie your own net (III.2).</strong> Since bondage is reflexive (\"<em>ahaṃ saḥ, mama idam</em>\"), watch for the two threads of the knot: the <strong>identity-claim</strong> (\"I am this\") and the <strong>possession-claim</strong> (\"this is mine\"). Loosening either slackens the net the bird wove around itself.","4. <strong>Iron, not fire (III.3).</strong> Under the hammer of circumstance, recall: the blows fall on the <em>iron</em> (the conditioned self), never on the <em>fire</em> (consciousness). The simile is a refuge in adversity — \"I am the unbeaten fire pervading the beaten ball.\"","5. <strong>Run the guṇa-audit (III.5).</strong> Take Maitri's two lists as a diagnostic checklist. Tamas-symptoms (dullness, despondency, sloth) call for <em>rajas</em> (activity, effort); rajas-symptoms (craving, agitation, the love–hate axis) call for <em>sattva</em> (the tapas-purification prescribed in IV.3). Identify which guṇa is \"overpowering\" before choosing the counter-measure.","6. <strong>Watch the love–hate axis (III.5).</strong> Maitri singles out <em>dveṣa</em> (aversion to the unwanted) and <em>abhiṣvaṅga</em> (clinging to the wanted) as the climactic pair — the rebirth-engine. Observe how the mind ceaselessly sorts experience into \"want / don't-want\"; the cessation of this sorting is the <em>amanībhāva</em> (mind-lessness) that IV.7 will call \"the supreme state.\""],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka IV — The Remedy: Tapas, the Purified Mind, and the Guṇa-Cosmos","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka IV","title":"The Remedy: Tapas, the Purified Mind, and the Guṇa-Cosmos","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Having diagnosed bondage (III), the Upaniṣad gives the cure (</em>pratividhi<em>). The world's objects are unmasked as a chain of illusion-similes; the path is laid out as Veda-knowledge, svadharma, and tapas, by which</em> sattva <em>is won,</em> sattva <em>yielding mind, mind yielding the Self. The teaching then crystallizes in a block of fifteen ślokas — the celebrated</em> cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ <em>(\"the mind alone is saṃsāra\") cluster and the Kautsāyani-stuti hymn — and closes with the guṇa-cosmology in which the one conscious \"part\" unfolds as Brahmā, Rudra, and Viṣṇu.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"IV.1","title":"","sa":["ते ह खल्वथोर्ध्वरेतसोऽतिविस्मिता अतिसमेत्योचुः — भगवन्नमस्ते, त्वं नः शाधि, त्वमस्माकं गतिरन्या न विद्यत इति । अस्य कोऽतिथिर्भूतात्मनो येनेदं हित्वामुन्येव सायुज्यमुपैति — इति तान्होवाच ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["te ha khalv athordhva-retaso 'tivismitā atisametyocuḥ — bhagavan namas te, tvaṃ naḥ śādhi, tvam asmākaṃ gatir anyā na vidyata iti | asya ko 'tithir bhūtātmano yenedaṃ hitvāmuny eva sāyujyam upaiti — iti tān hovāca ‖"],"words":[["ūrdhva-retasaḥ ati-vismitāḥ","nom. pl.","\"the celibates, greatly amazed\""],["ati-sametya ūcuḥ","abs. + pf.","\"drawing near, said\""],["namaḥ te, tvaṃ naḥ śādhi","\"homage to you; instruct us\"","\"homage! teach us\""],["tvam asmākaṃ gatiḥ, anyā na vidyate","nom.","\"you are our refuge; no other is found\""],["kaḥ atithiḥ bhūtātmanaḥ","nom. + gen.","\"what is the resort (lit. 'guest-way'/means) of the elemental self\""],["yena idaṃ hitvā amunā eva sāyujyam upaiti","instr. + abs. + acc.","\"by which, abandoning this, it attains union with that (Self)\""]],"lit":"Then the celibate (sages), greatly amazed, drew near and said: \"Lord, homage to you! Teach us; you are our refuge — no other is found. What is the resort of this elemental self, by which — abandoning this (lower self) — it attains union (<em>sāyujya</em>) with that (supreme Self)?\" To them he said (this).","para":"The pupils, having heard the diagnosis (III), ask the therapeutic question: by what <em>means</em> (here <em>atithi</em>, lit. \"way of approach,\" read by Cowell as \"resort/refuge\") does the <em>bhūtātman</em> abandon its bound condition and attain <em>sāyujya</em>, union with the higher Self? Prapāṭhaka IV is the answer.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","glosses <em>atithi</em> as <em>gantavya-mārga</em>, the path to be travelled — the <em>pratividhi</em> of IV.3"],["E. B. Cowell","flags <em>atithi</em> as a difficult word, \"the means of approach.\""],["S. Radhakrishnan","notes the goal-word <em>sāyujya</em> (union/communion), a term of later theistic Vedānta, marking Maitri's bridge toward bhakti"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"IV.2","title":"","sa":["अथान्यत्राप्युक्तम् — महानदीष्वूर्मय इव निवर्तकमस्य यत्पुराकृतम्, समुद्रवेलेव दुर्निवार्यमस्य मृत्योरागमनम्, सदसत्फलमयैर्हि पाशैः पशुरिव बद्धम्, बन्धनस्थस्येवास्वातन्त्र्यम्, यमविषयस्थस्येव बहुभयावस्थम्, मदिरोन्मत्त इवामोदं मदिरोन्मत्तम्, पाप्मना गृहीत इव भ्राम्यमाणम्, महोरगदष्ट इव विषदृष्टम्, महान्धकार इव रागान्धम्, इन्द्रजालमिव मायामयम्, स्वप्नमिव मिथ्यादर्शनम्, कदलीगर्भ इवासारम्, नट इव क्षणवेषम्, चित्रभित्तिरिव मिथ्यामनोरमम् — इत्यथोक्तम् ॥ शब्दस्पर्शादयो येऽर्था अनर्था इव ते स्थिताः । येष्वासक्तस्तु भूतात्मा न स्मरेच्च परं पदम् ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["athānyatrāpy uktam — mahā-nadīṣv ūrmaya iva nivartakam asya yat purā-kṛtam, samudra-veleva durnivāryam asya mṛtyor āgamanam, sad-asat-phala-mayair hi pāśaiḥ paśur iva baddham, bandhana-sthasyevāsvātantryam, yama-viṣaya-sthasyeva bahu-bhayāvasthaṃ, madironmatta ivāmodaṃ madironmattaṃ, pāpmanā gṛhīta iva bhrāmyamāṇaṃ, mahoraga-daṣṭa iva viṣa-dṛṣṭaṃ, mahāndhakāra iva rāgāndhaṃ, indrajālam iva māyāmayaṃ, svapnam iva mithyā-darśanaṃ, kadalī-garbha iväsāraṃ, naṭa iva kṣaṇa-veṣaṃ, citra-bhittir iva mithyā-manoramam — ity athoktam ‖ śabda-sparśādayo ye 'rthā anarthā iva te sthitāḥ | yeṣv āsaktas tu bhūtātmā na smarec ca paraṃ padam ‖"],"words":[["mahā-nadīṣu ūrmayaḥ iva nivartakam","loc. + nom. + n.","\"irreversible, like waves in great rivers\""],["asya yat purā-kṛtam","gen. + n.","\"what was formerly done by him (past karma)\""],["samudra-velā iva durnivāryam … mṛtyoḥ āgamanam","nom. + n.","\"the coming of his death, hard to ward off, like the sea-tide\""],["sad-asat-phala-mayaiḥ pāśaiḥ paśuḥ iva baddham","instr. + nom.","\"bound like a beast by cords made of good-and-bad fruits\""],["bandhana-sthasya iva a-svātantryam","gen. + n.","\"lacking freedom, like a prisoner\""],["yama-viṣaya-sthasya iva bahu-bhaya-avastham","gen. + n.","\"in a state of many fears, as one in Yama's realm\""],["madirā-unmattaḥ iva …","nom.","\"as one drunk on wine — (deludedly) elated, intoxicated\""],["pāpmanā gṛhītaḥ iva bhrāmyamāṇam","instr. + part.","\"whirled about, as if seized by an evil spirit\""],["mahā-uraga-daṣṭaḥ iva viṣa-dṛṣṭam","nom.","\"(its) sight poisoned, as if bitten by a great serpent\""],["mahā-andhakāraḥ iva rāga-andham","nom.","\"blinded by passion, as if in great darkness\""],["indra-jālam iva māyā-mayam","nom.","\"illusory, like a conjuror's trick\""],["svapnam iva mithyā-darśanam","nom.","\"a false appearance, like a dream\""],["kadalī-garbhaḥ iva a-sāram","nom.","\"pithless, like the plantain's core\""],["naṭaḥ iva kṣaṇa-veṣam","nom.","\"(changing) momentary costume, like an actor\""],["citra-bhittiḥ iva mithyā-manoramam","nom.","\"falsely charming, like a painted wall\""],["śabda-sparśa-ādayaḥ ye arthāḥ anarthāḥ iva","nom. pl.","\"the objects — sound, touch, and the rest — stand as if (real) things, (though) non-things\""],["yeṣu āsaktaḥ bhūtātmā na smaret paraṃ padam","loc. + nom.","\"attached to which, the elemental self remembers not the supreme state\""]],"lit":"\"So too it is said elsewhere: 'Irreversible, like the waves of great rivers, is what one has done before; hard to ward off, like the sea-tide, is the coming of one's death; bound like a beast by cords made of good and bad fruits; without independence, like one in fetters; full of many fears, like one in Yama's domain; intoxicated as by wine, foolishly elated; whirled about as if possessed by an evil spirit; its vision poisoned, as if bitten by a great serpent; blinded by passion, as in great darkness; illusory, like a conjuror's show; a false appearance, like a dream; pithless, like the plantain-stalk's core; (clad in) momentary costume, like an actor; falsely delightful, like a painted wall.' And so it is said: 'Sound, touch, and the rest — those objects which are (really) non-things — stand as though (real); attached to which, the elemental self remembers not the supreme state.'\"","para":"A cascade of fourteen similes for the bound condition and the unreality of its objects — the longest such <em>upamā</em>-chain in the Upaniṣads, and a clear ancestor of the illusion-imagery of later Advaita (the conjuror's trick, the dream, the painted wall, the pithless plantain). The closing śloka states the thesis: sense-objects (<em>śabda, sparśa,</em> etc.) are <em>anartha</em> — \"non-things\"/calamities — that masquerade as real (<em>arthāḥ ... anarthāḥ iva</em>), and the self enthralled by them \"remembers not the supreme state\" (<em>paraṃ padam</em>). Memory/forgetting of the <em>paraṃ padam</em> is, for Maitri, the hinge of bondage and release.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","reads the simile-chain as <em>jagan-mithyātva</em> avant la lettre — the world's seeming-reality is <em>māyāmaya</em>, dream-like"],["E. B. Cowell","admire the rhetorical accumulation and note the <em>kadalī-garbha</em> (\"plantain-pith\") and <em>indra-jāla</em> (\"net of Indra,\" conjuring) images that Advaita inherits"],["Paul Deussen","highlights <em>anarthāḥ</em> — the objects are not merely unreal but <em>harmful</em> (an-artha = \"non-good\")"],["S. Radhakrishnan","stresses the diagnostic <em>na smaret paraṃ padam</em>: bondage is <em>forgetting</em>, liberation is <em>re-collection</em> of the Self"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"IV.3","title":"","sa":["अयं वाव खल्वस्य प्रतिविधिर्भूतात्मनः — यद्विद्याधिगमः, स्वस्य धर्मस्यानुचरणम्, स्वाश्रमेष्वानुक्रमणम् । स्वधर्म एव सर्वं धत्ते, स्तम्भशाखेवेतराणि; अनेनोर्ध्वभाग्भवति, अन्यथा अधः पतति । एष स्वधर्माभिभूतो यो वेदेषु; न स्वधर्मातिक्रमेणाश्रमी भवति; आश्रमेष्वेवावस्थितस्तपस्वी चेत्युच्यते । एतदप्युक्तम् — नातपस्कस्यात्मध्यानेऽधिगमः कर्मशुद्धिर्वा — इत्येवं ह्याह ॥ तपसा प्राप्यते सत्त्वं सत्त्वात्सम्प्राप्यते मनः । मनसा प्राप्यते त्वात्मा ह्यात्मापत्त्या निवर्तत इति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["ayaṃ vāva khalv asya pratividhir bhūtātmanaḥ — yad vidyādhigamaḥ, svasya dharmasyānucaraṇaṃ, svāśrameṣv ānukramaṇam | svadharma eva sarvaṃ dhatte, stambha-śākheva itarāṇi; anenordhva-bhāg bhavati, anyathādhaḥ patati | eṣa svadharmābhibhūto yo vedeṣu; na svadharmātikrameṇāśramī bhavati; āśrameṣv evāvasthitas tapasvī cety ucyate | etad apy uktam — nātapaskasyātma-dhyāne 'dhigamaḥ karma-śuddhir vā — ity evaṃ hy āha ‖ tapasā prāpyate sattvaṃ sattvāt samprāpyate manaḥ | manasā prāpyate tv ātmā hy ātmāpattyā nivartata iti ‖"],"words":[["pratividhiḥ bhūtātmanaḥ","nom. + gen.","\"the remedy for the elemental self\""],["vidyā-adhigamaḥ","nom.","\"the attainment of (sacred) knowledge\""],["svasya dharmasya anucaraṇam","gen. + n.","\"the practice of one's own dharma\""],["sva-āśrameṣu ānukramaṇam","loc. + n.","\"the orderly passage through one's own life-stages\""],["svadharmaḥ eva sarvaṃ dhatte","nom. + acc. + pres.","\"one's own dharma upholds the whole\""],["stambha-śākhā iva itarāṇi","nom.","\"(supports) the rest, as a trunk its branches\""],["ūrdhva-bhāk / adhaḥ patati","nom. / pres.","\"by it one goes upward; otherwise one falls down\""],["āśrameṣu avasthitaḥ tapasvī","loc. + nom.","\"established in the āśramas, an ascetic\""],["na atapaskasya ātma-dhyāne adhigamaḥ","gen. + loc.","\"for the non-ascetic, no attainment in Self-meditation\""],["karma-śuddhiḥ vā","nom.","\"nor purity through works\""],["tapasā prāpyate sattvam","instr. + pres. pass.","\"by tapas, sattva is won\""],["sattvāt samprāpyate manaḥ","abl. + pres.","\"from sattva, mind is fully won\""],["manasā prāpyate tu ātmā","instr.","\"by mind, the Self is reached\""],["ātma-āpattyā nivartate","instr. + pres.","\"by attaining the Self, (the round) ceases\""]],"lit":"\"This, verily, is the remedy for the elemental self: the attainment of (sacred) knowledge; the practice of one's own dharma; orderly progress through one's own life-stages. One's own dharma upholds everything, as the trunk (upholds) the branches; by it one becomes upward-tending — otherwise one falls down. He who (lives) by the Vedas is upheld by his own dharma; not by transgressing his own dharma does one become an <em>āśramin</em>; established in the āśramas, an ascetic — so is he called. And this too is said: 'For the non-ascetic there is no attainment in meditation on the Self, nor purification through works.' For thus it is said: '<em>By tapas is sattva won; from sattva, mind is gained; by the mind the Self is reached; and by attaining the Self, one returns no more.</em>'\"","para":"The <em>pratividhi</em> in three components — <strong>knowledge</strong> (<em>vidyā</em>), <strong>svadharma</strong> (right action in one's station), and <strong>the āśrama-discipline</strong> crowned by <strong>tapas</strong>. Maitri insists on the ethical-ascetic precondition: meditation bears no fruit for the <em>atapaska</em> (the non-ascetic). The capping śloka gives the soteriological ladder in four rungs: <strong>tapas → sattva → manas → ātman → nivṛtti</strong> (no return). Austerity purifies the guṇa-balance toward <em>sattva</em>; sattva clarifies the mind; the clarified mind reaches the Self; and Self-attainment (<em>ātmāpatti</em>) ends the round of rebirth. This verse is the structural spine of the whole remedy.","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","reads the four-rung verse as the <em>krama</em> (graded sequence): tapas is the means, <em>ātmāpatti</em> the end; \"<em>nivartate</em>\" = the cessation of saṃsāra. He stresses that <em>vidyā</em> without <em>adhikāra</em> (the ethical-ascetic fitness) is barren"],["E. B. Cowell","notes the strongly orthoprax tone (svadharma, āśrama, Veda) — Maitri reconciles ritual-dharma with jñāna"],["Paul Deussen","maps tapas→sattva→manas→ātman onto a Sāṃkhya-Yoga purification of the <em>antaḥkaraṇa</em>"],["S. Radhakrishnan","calls IV.3 the verse where Maitri joins <em>karma-mārga</em> and <em>jñāna-mārga</em>: discipline first, then the mind, then the Self"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"IV.4","title":"The fifteen ślokas","sa":["(1) यथा निरिन्धनो वह्निः स्वयोनावुपशाम्यति । तथा वृत्तिक्षयाच्चित्तं स्वयोनावुपशाम्यति ॥","(2) स्वयोनावुपशान्तस्य मनसः सत्यगामिनः । इन्द्रियार्थाविमूढस्यानृताः कर्मवशानुगाः ॥","(3) चित्तमेव हि संसारस्तत्प्रयत्नेन शोधयेत् । यच्चित्तस्तन्मयो भवति गुह्यमेतत्सनातनम् ॥","(4) चित्तस्य हि प्रसादेन हन्ति कर्म शुभाशुभम् । प्रसन्नात्मात्मनि स्थित्वा सुखमव्ययमश्नुते ॥","(5) समासक्तं यदा चित्तं जन्तोर्विषयगोचरे । यद्येवं ब्रह्मणि स्यात्तत्को न मुच्येत बन्धनात् ॥","(6) मनो हि द्विविधं प्रोक्तं शुद्धं चाशुद्धमेव च । अशुद्धं कामसङ्कल्पं शुद्धं कामविवर्जितम् ॥","(7) लयविक्षेपरहितं मनः कृत्वा सुनिश्चलम् । यदा यात्यमनीभावं तदा तत्परमं पदम् ॥","(8) तावदेव निरोद्धव्यं हृदि यावत्क्षयं गतम् । एतज्ज्ञानं च मोक्षं च शेषास्तु ग्रन्थविस्तराः ॥","(9) समाधिनिर्धूतमलस्य चेतसो निवेशितस्यात्मनि यत्सुखं लभेत् । न शक्यते वर्णयितुं गिरा तदा स्वयं तदन्तःकरणेन गृह्यते ॥","(10) अपामपोऽग्निरग्नौ वा व्योम्नि व्योम न लक्षयेत् । एवमन्तर्गतं चित्तं पुरुषः प्रतिमुच्यते ॥","(11) मन एव मनुष्याणां कारणं बन्धमोक्षयोः । बन्धाय विषयासक्तं मुक्त्यै निर्विषयं स्मृतम् ॥","(12) त्वं ब्रह्मा त्वं च वै विष्णुस्त्वं रुद्रस्त्वं प्रजापतिः । त्वमग्निर्वरुणो वायुस्त्वमिन्द्रस्त्वं निशाकरः ॥","(13) त्वं मनुस्त्वं यमश्च त्वं पृथिवी त्वमथाच्युतः । स्वार्थे स्वाभाविकेऽर्थे च बहुधा तिष्ठसे दिवि ॥","(14) विश्वेश्वर नमस्तुभ्यं विश्वात्मा विश्वकर्मकृत् । विश्वभुग्विश्वमायुस्त्वं विश्वक्रीडारतिः प्रभुः ॥","(15) नमः शान्तात्मने तुभ्यं नमो गुह्यतमाय च । अचिन्त्यायाप्रमेयाय अनादिनिधनाय च ॥"],"iast":["yathā nirindhano vahniḥ svayonāv upaśāmyati | tathā vṛtti-kṣayāc cittaṃ svayonāv upaśāmyati ‖","svayonāv upaśāntasya manasaḥ satya-gāminaḥ | indriyārthāvimūḍhasyānṛtāḥ karma-vaśānugāḥ ‖","cittam eva hi saṃsāras tat prayatnena śodhayet | yac-cittas tan-mayo bhavati guhyam etat sanātanam ‖","cittasya hi prasādena hanti karma śubhāśubham | prasannātmātmani sthitvā sukham avyayam aśnute ‖","samāsaktaṃ yadā cittaṃ jantor viṣaya-gocare | yady evaṃ brahmaṇi syāt tat ko na mucyeta bandhanāt ‖","mano hi dvividhaṃ proktaṃ śuddhaṃ cāśuddham eva ca | aśuddhaṃ kāma-saṅkalpaṃ śuddhaṃ kāma-vivarjitam ‖","laya-vikṣepa-rahitaṃ manaḥ kṛtvā suniścalam | yadā yāty amanībhāvaṃ tadā tat paramaṃ padam ‖","tāvad eva niroddhavyaṃ hṛdi yāvat kṣayaṃ gatam | etaj jñānaṃ ca mokṣaṃ ca śeṣās tu grantha-vistarāḥ ‖","samādhi-nirdhūta-malasya cetaso niveśitasyātmani yat sukhaṃ labhet | na śakyate varṇayituṃ girā tadā svayaṃ tad-antaḥkaraṇena gṛhyate ‖","apām apo 'gnir agnau vā vyomni vyoma na lakṣayet | evam antar-gataṃ cittaṃ puruṣaḥ pratimucyate ‖","mana eva manuṣyāṇāṃ kāraṇaṃ bandha-mokṣayoḥ | bandhāya viṣayāsaktaṃ muktyai nirviṣayaṃ smṛtam ‖","tvaṃ brahmā tvaṃ ca vai viṣṇus tvaṃ rudras tvaṃ prajāpatiḥ | tvam agnir varuṇo vāyus tvam indras tvaṃ niśākaraḥ ‖","tvaṃ manus tvaṃ yamaś ca tvaṃ pṛthivī tvam athācyutaḥ | svārthe svābhāvike 'rthe ca bahudhā tiṣṭhase divi ‖","viśveśvara namas tubhyaṃ viśvātmā viśva-karma-kṛt | viśva-bhug viśva-māyus tvaṃ viśva-krīḍā-ratiḥ prabhuḥ ‖","namaḥ śāntātmane tubhyaṃ namo guhyatamāya ca | acintyāyāprameyāya anādi-nidhanāya ca ‖"],"words":[["cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ","nom.","\"the mind alone is saṃsāra\""],["tat prayatnena śodhayet","acc. + instr. + opt.","\"let one purify it with effort\""],["yac-cittaḥ tan-mayaḥ bhavati","nom.","\"of whatever mind (one is), that one becomes\""],["guhyam etat sanātanam","n. nom.","\"this is the eternal secret\""],["manaḥ dvividhaṃ proktam","nom. + p.p.","\"mind is declared twofold\""],["śuddhaṃ ca aśuddham eva ca","nom.","\"pure, and impure\""],["aśuddhaṃ kāma-saṅkalpam","nom.","\"the impure = (mind) with desire-and-intention\""],["śuddhaṃ kāma-vivarjitam","nom.","\"the pure = (mind) free of desire\""],["laya-vikṣepa-rahitam","acc.","\"freed of torpor (laya) and distraction (vikṣepa)\""],["manaḥ kṛtvā suniścalam","acc. + abs.","\"having made the mind perfectly still\""],["amanī-bhāvaṃ yāti","acc. + pres.","\"reaches the mind-less state\""],["tat paramaṃ padam","n. nom.","\"that is the supreme state\""],["manaḥ eva manuṣyāṇāṃ kāraṇam","nom. + gen.","\"mind alone is the cause for human beings\""],["bandha-mokṣayoḥ","gen. du.","\"of bondage and of release\""],["bandhāya viṣaya-āsaktam","dat. + acc.","\"attached to objects → for bondage\""],["muktyai nirviṣayam smṛtam","dat. + n.","\"object-free → for release, it is taught\""]],"lit":"— \"As a fire without fuel dies down in its own source, so the mind (<em>citta</em>), through the cessation of its modifications (<em>vṛtti-kṣaya</em>), is quenched in its own source.\" <em>(The mind, deprived of the \"fuel\" of objects, subsides into its origin. Note the term</em> vṛtti-kṣaya<em>, identical with Yoga-Sūtra 1.2's</em> citta-vṛtti-nirodha<em>.)</em>","para":"of IV.4. The eleven mind-ślokas argue a single thesis — <em>the mind is the whole of saṃsāra and of mokṣa</em> — and prescribe its purification (subtract <em>kāma-saṅkalpa</em>), stilling (free of <em>laya</em> and <em>vikṣepa</em>), and final dissolution (<em>amanī-bhāva</em>) in the Self, a merging as seamless as water poured in water. The Kautsāyani-stuti then turns from analysis to adoration: the Self purified into is the <em>one</em> behind every god — Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, the elements, the Moon, Manu, Yama — the \"Lord whose delight is the world's play,\" yet in itself <em>śāntātman</em>, hidden, unthinkable, beginningless. Thus the remedy ends where bhakti and jñāna coincide.","coms":[["Commentary","digest (IV.4).<strong> </strong>Rt<strong> treats verse 3 (<em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em>) and verse 11 (<em>mana eva … bandha-mokṣayoḥ</em>) as the <em>mahā-vākyas</em> of the section, the rest as their expansion; <em>amanī-bhāva</em> (v. 7) he equates with <em>manonāśa</em>, the dissolution of mind taught in the Yoga tradition. </strong>Cw<strong> documents the heavy overlap with the </strong>Amṛtabindu / Brahmabindu Upaniṣad<strong> (vv. 3, 6, 11 especially) and the </strong>Yoga texts<strong>, leaving open which borrowed from which. </strong>De<strong> reads the eleven verses as a compact Yoga-treatise embedded in Maitri (the <em>vṛtti-kṣaya</em> of v. 1 = YS 1.2). </strong>Rk<strong> sees the <em>stuti</em> as Maitri's theistic resolution: the impersonal <em>paraṃ padam</em> of the mind-verses and the personal \"<em>tvam</em>\" of the hymn are the same reality under jñāna and bhakti. </strong>Hu renders the <em>stuti</em> as a litany of the \"Thou art all\" type, comparable to Śvetāśvatara 4."]],"notes":["<em>The text now says</em> atraite ślokā bhavanti <em>(\"here are these ślokas\") and gives eleven verses on the mind (the</em> cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ <em>cluster), followed by the four-verse</em> Kautsāyani-stuti<em>. All fifteen stand under the single section-number \"‖ 4 ‖.\"</em>","— \"For the mind quieted in its own source, bound for the truth, undeluded by sense-objects — the deeds that follow the sway of (past) karma become as unreal (<em>anṛta</em>).\" <em>(Once the mind is truth-bound, the momentum of old karma loses its grip and is seen as unreal.)</em>","— \"<strong>The mind alone is saṃsāra; let one purify it with effort. What one's mind is, that one becomes — this is the eternal secret.</strong>\" <em>(Maitri's most-quoted verse, and the seed of the entire \"mind-only\" current in later Vedānta and Yoga. Cf. its near-twin at Amṛtabindu 2 —</em> mana eva manuṣyāṇāṃ kāraṇaṃ bandha-mokṣayoḥ <em>— which Maitri itself gives below as verse 11.)</em>","— \"By the serenity (<em>prasāda</em>) of the mind one destroys karma, good and bad alike; with serene self, abiding in the Self, one enjoys imperishable bliss.\" <em>(Cf. Bhagavad-Gītā 2.64–65, the</em> prasāda<em>-bliss link.)</em>","— \"When a creature's mind is (so) fastened on the field of sense-objects — if it were thus (fastened) on Brahman, who would not be freed from bondage?\" <em>(The wry pivot-verse: redirect the very intensity of attachment from objects to Brahman, and liberation is certain.)</em>","— \"Mind is declared twofold: pure and impure. The impure is (the mind) of desire-and-resolve; the pure is (the mind) freed of desire.\" <em>(The purification of IV.3's ladder is here defined: it is the subtraction of</em> kāma-saṅkalpa<em>.)</em>","— \"Having made the mind perfectly still, freed of torpor and distraction — when it reaches the mind-less state (<em>amanī-bhāva</em>), then is the supreme state.\" <em>(The twin enemies of meditation,</em> laya <em>(sinking/sleep) and</em> vikṣepa <em>(scattering), named centuries before the Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha and Gauḍapāda made the pair standard.)</em>","— \"Just so long must (the mind) be checked in the heart, until it goes to dissolution. This is knowledge and this is liberation; all the rest is mere extension of books (<em>grantha-vistara</em>).\" <em>(A famously austere verse: the whole of liberating knowledge is the dissolution of mind in the heart; the rest is bookish elaboration.)</em>","— \"The bliss that the mind attains when, its stains shaken off by samādhi, it is settled in the Self — that cannot be told in speech; it is grasped, then, by the inner organ itself.\" <em>(The bliss of the Self is</em> anirvacanīya <em>— ineffable, known only by direct inner apprehension. Cf. Taittirīya 2.4,</em> yato vāco nivartante<em>.)</em>","— \"Water in water, fire in fire, or ether in ether — one could not tell them apart; even so, he whose mind has gone within — that Person is liberated.\" <em>(Three \"indistinguishables\" for the merging of the purified mind in the Self — the drop-in-the-ocean image in its sharpest Maitri form.)</em>","— \"<strong>Mind alone is the cause, for human beings, of bondage and of release: attached to objects, (it makes) for bondage; freed of objects, it is held (to make) for release.</strong>\" <em>(The summation of the eleven mind-ślokas — and, verbatim, the second verse of the Amṛtabindu Upaniṣad, the canonical opener of this whole document-series. Maitri is one of its likely sources.)</em>","<strong>The Kautsāyani-stuti</strong> — <em>atha yatheyaṃ kautsāyani-stutiḥ</em>: \"And here is the praise uttered by Kautsāyana.\" A four-verse theistic hymn identifying the one Self with the whole pantheon — the devotional crown of the remedy.","— \"You are Brahmā, and you indeed are Viṣṇu; you are Rudra, you Prajāpati; you are Agni, Varuṇa, Vāyu; you are Indra, you the Moon.\"","— \"You are Manu, you are Yama, you are the Earth, and you are the Imperishable; for your own sake and in your own nature, manifoldly you abide in heaven.\"","— \"O Lord of all, homage to you! — Self of all, doer of every work, enjoyer of all, life of all; you are the Lord whose delight is the play of the universe (<em>viśva-krīḍā-rati</em>).\"","— \"Homage to you, the tranquil-souled; homage to the most hidden; to the unthinkable, the immeasurable, the one without beginning or end.\""]},{"ref":"IV.5","title":"","sa":["तमो वा इदमेकमास, तत्परेणेरितं विषमत्वं प्रयाति, एतद्वै रजसो रूपम्; तद्रजः खल्वीरितं विषमत्वं प्रयाति, एतद्वै तमसो रूपम्; तत्तमः खल्वीरितं सम्प्रास्रवति, एतद्वै सत्त्वस्य रूपम्; तत्सत्त्वमेवेरितं, तत्सत्त्वात्सम्प्रास्रवत् सोऽंशोऽयं यश्चेतनमात्रः प्रतिपुरुषं क्षेत्रज्ञः सङ्कल्पाध्यवसायाभिमानलिङ्गः प्रजापतिः । तस्य प्रोक्ता अग्र्यास्तनवो ब्रह्मा रुद्रो विष्णुरिति । अथ यो ह खलु वावास्य राजसोंऽशोऽसौ स योऽयं ब्रह्मा; अथ यो ह खलु वावास्य तामसोंऽशोऽसौ स योऽयं रुद्रः; अथ यो ह खलु वावास्य सात्त्विकोंऽशोऽसौ स एष विष्णुः । स वा एष एकस्त्रिधाभूतोऽष्टधैकादशधा द्वादशधापरिमितधा चोद्भूतः, उद्भूतत्वाद्भूतेषु चरति, प्रतिष्ठा सर्वभूतानामधिपतिर्बभूव — इत्यसावात्मान्तर्बहिश्चान्तर्बहिश्च ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["tamo vā idam ekam āsa, tat pareṇeritaṃ viṣamatvaṃ prayāti, etad vai rajaso rūpam; tad rajaḥ khalv īritaṃ viṣamatvaṃ prayāti, etad vai tamaso rūpam; tat tamaḥ khalv īritaṃ samprāsravati, etad vai sattvasya rūpam; tat sattvam everitaṃ, tat sattvāt samprāsravat so 'ṃśo 'yaṃ yaś cetana-mātraḥ pratipuruṣaṃ kṣetrajñaḥ saṅkalpādhyavasāyābhimāna-liṅgaḥ prajāpatiḥ | tasya proktā agryās tanavo brahmā rudro viṣṇur iti | atha yo ha khalu vāvāsya rājaso 'ṃśo 'sau sa yo 'yaṃ brahmā; atha yo ha khalu vāvāsya tāmaso 'ṃśo 'sau sa yo 'yaṃ rudraḥ; atha yo ha khalu vāvāsya sāttviko 'ṃśo 'sau sa eṣa viṣṇuḥ | sa vā eṣa ekas tridhā-bhūto 'ṣṭadhaikādaśadhā dvādaśadhāparimitadhā codbhūtaḥ, udbhūtatvād bhūteṣu carati, pratiṣṭhā sarva-bhūtānām adhipatir babhūva — ity asāv ātmāntar bahiś cāntar bahiś ca ‖"],"words":[["tamaḥ vā idam ekam āsa","nom. + impf.","\"darkness (tamas) alone, verily, was this in the beginning\""],["pareṇa īritam","instr. + p.p.","\"set in motion by the Supreme\""],["viṣamatvaṃ prayāti","acc. + pres.","\"goes to unevenness (loss of equilibrium)\""],["etad rajasaḥ rūpam","n. nom.","\"this is the form of rajas\""],["samprāsravati","pres.","\"flows forth / exudes\""],["etad sattvasya rūpam","n. nom.","\"this is the form of sattva\""],["saḥ aṃśaḥ … cetana-mātraḥ","nom.","\"that 'part' which is pure consciousness\""],["prati-puruṣaṃ kṣetrajñaḥ","adv. + nom.","\"the field-knower in each person\""],["saṅkalpa-adhyavasāya-abhimāna-liṅgaḥ prajāpatiḥ","nom.","\"marked by resolve-decision-conceit, (the) Prajāpati\""],["proktāḥ agryāḥ tanavaḥ","nom. pl.","\"his foremost embodiments are declared (to be)\""],["brahmā rudraḥ viṣṇuḥ","nom.","\"Brahmā, Rudra, Viṣṇu\""],["rājasaḥ aṃśaḥ … brahmā","nom.","\"his rajas-part is Brahmā\""],["tāmasaḥ aṃśaḥ … rudraḥ","nom.","\"his tamas-part is Rudra\""],["sāttvikaḥ aṃśaḥ … viṣṇuḥ","nom.","\"his sattva-part is Viṣṇu\""],["ekaḥ tridhā-bhūtaḥ aṣṭadhā ekādaśadhā dvādaśadhā aparimitadhā udbhūtaḥ","nom.","\"being one, arisen as threefold, eightfold, elevenfold, twelvefold, infinite-fold\""],["bhūteṣu carati","loc. + pres.","\"moves among the beings\""],["pratiṣṭhā sarva-bhūtānām adhipatiḥ babhūva","nom. + gen. + pf.","\"became the support and overlord of all beings\""],["ātmā antar bahiś ca","nom.","\"the Self, within and without\""]],"lit":"\"Darkness (<em>tamas</em>), verily, was this One in the beginning. That, impelled by the Supreme, goes to unevenness — this is the form of <em>rajas</em>. That <em>rajas</em>, impelled, goes to unevenness — this is (in turn) the form of <em>tamas</em>. That <em>tamas</em>, impelled, exudes (forth) — this is the form of <em>sattva</em>. That <em>sattva</em> alone, being impelled — out of that <em>sattva</em> there flows forth that 'part' which is pure consciousness, the field-knower in each person, marked by resolve, decision, and self-conceit: Prajāpati. His foremost embodiments are declared (to be) Brahmā, Rudra, Viṣṇu. Now that <em>rajas</em>-part of his — that is Brahmā; that <em>tamas</em>-part of his — that is Rudra; that <em>sattva</em>-part of his — that is Viṣṇu. He, verily, being One, arisen as threefold, eightfold, elevenfold, twelvefold, and infinite-fold — by reason of his arising, moves among the beings; the support and overlord of all beings he became. Thus that Self is within and without — within and without.\"","para":"The cosmology that grounds the whole guṇa-psychology of III. In the beginning was undifferentiated <em>tamas</em> (the unmanifest equilibrium); impelled by \"the Supreme\" (<em>para</em>), it loses equilibrium (<em>viṣamatva</em>) and the guṇa-cycle turns — <em>tamas → rajas → sattva</em> — until from purified <em>sattva</em> \"flows forth\" the conscious <strong>aṃśa</strong> (the same <em>kṣetrajña</em> of II.5, here named <strong>Prajāpati</strong>). His three \"foremost embodiments\" are the Trimūrti, mapped to the guṇas: <strong>Brahmā = rajas</strong> (creative activity), <strong>Rudra = tamas</strong> (dissolution), <strong>Viṣṇu = sattva</strong> (preservation/luminosity). The one Self then multiplies (threefold … twelvefold … infinite-fold — the gods, the elements, the senses, the months/Ādityas), pervading all beings as their support and overlord, \"within and without.\"","coms":[["Rāmatīrtha","reads the passage as the <em>sṛṣṭi-krama</em> (order of emanation): the guṇas' loss of equilibrium begins manifestation, and the Trimūrti are the guṇa-functions of the one Prajāpati. He defends the <em>tamas-first</em> sequence as the unmanifest <em>mūla-prakṛti</em> preceding the rise of rajas and sattva"],["E. B. Cowell","marks the textual corruption and supplies the Trimūrti-guṇa equation (Brahmā-rajas, Rudra-tamas, Viṣṇu-sattva) as Maitri's distinctive contribution — one of the earliest explicit guṇa-mappings of the three gods"],["Paul Deussen","stresses the Sāṃkhya <em>guṇa-kṣobha</em> (the \"agitation\" of the guṇas) as the trigger of cosmogony, here given a theistic <em>para</em> as impeller"],["S. Radhakrishnan","notes the synthesis: a Sāṃkhya guṇa-emanation, a Vedāntic one-Self, and a Purāṇic Trimūrti are fused — and the closing <em>antar bahiś ca</em> (\"within and without\") restores the immanent–transcendent Self of the Īśā and Muṇḍaka, sealing the philosophical core of the Upaniṣad"]],"notes":["<em>Textual note.</em> The opening clauses (<em>tamas → rajas → tamas → sattva</em>) are awkwardly transmitted in this e-text (the <em>viṣayatva/viṣamatva</em> and repeated <em>tamasaḥ rūpam</em> are widely recognized as corrupt; Cowell and van Buitenen reconstruct a cleaner <em>tamas → rajas → sattva</em> progression). The translation above follows the transmitted words while the paraphrase gives the reconstructed sense."]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka IV · Prapāṭhaka IV — Details","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka IV","title":"Details","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>The architecture of the cure.</strong> IV moves diagnosis → therapy → adoration → cosmology: the illusion-similes (IV.2) complete the diagnosis of III; the <em>pratividhi</em> (IV.3) gives the disciplined path with its <strong>tapas → sattva → manas → ātman</strong> ladder; the fifteen ślokas (IV.4) distil the path into the doctrine of mind and crown it with the bhakti-hymn; and IV.5 grounds the whole guṇa-economy in a cosmogony where the one Self unfolds as the guṇa-Trimūrti.","- <strong>\"Mind alone is saṃsāra.\"</strong> Verse IV.4.3 (<em>cittam eva hi saṃsāraḥ</em>) and IV.4.11 (<em>mana eva … bandha-mokṣayoḥ</em>) are among the most influential single verses of the Upaniṣadic corpus, feeding directly into the <strong>Amṛtabindu</strong> (whose vv. 1–2 are near-identical, and which is the canonical opening title of this very document-series), the Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha's <em>manonāśa</em> doctrine, and the Yoga-Sūtra's <em>citta-vṛtti-nirodha</em> (cf. <em>vṛtti-kṣaya</em>, IV.4.1).","- <strong>Laya and vikṣepa (IV.4.7).</strong> Maitri names meditation's two obstacles — <em>laya</em> (torpor/sinking toward sleep) and <em>vikṣepa</em> (scattering/distraction) — and the goal beyond both, <em>amanī-bhāva</em> (\"mind-less-ness\"). This pair becomes standard equipment in all later meditation manuals.","- <strong>The guṇa-Trimūrti (IV.5).</strong> The mapping <strong>Brahmā=rajas, Viṣṇu=sattva, Rudra=tamas</strong> is one of the earliest in Sanskrit literature; Maitri thereby integrates the emerging Purāṇic theology of the three gods into a Sāṃkhya-Vedānta frame, and the section closes with the immanent-transcendent refrain <em>antar bahiś ca</em>.","- <strong>Jñāna and bhakti reconciled.</strong> The juxtaposition — austere mind-only verses (IV.4.1–11) immediately followed by the adoring <em>tvam</em>-hymn (IV.4.12–15) — is deliberate: the impersonal <em>paraṃ padam</em> and the personal Lord are presented as one reality approached by two roads. This is the seam where Maitri most clearly anticipates later theistic Vedānta (<em>sāyujya</em>, IV.1)."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka IV · Prapāṭhaka IV — Visual representation","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka IV","title":"Visual representation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>THE REMEDY — from bound elemental self to union (sāyujya)\n\n  IV.2  DIAGNOSIS: 14 illusion-similes\n        river-waves · sea-tide · beast-in-cords · prisoner · Yama&#x27;s realm ·\n        drunkard · spirit-possessed · serpent-bitten · passion-blind ·\n        conjuror&#x27;s-trick · DREAM · pithless-plantain · actor · painted-wall\n        →  &quot;objects are anartha (non-things) that look like things&quot;\n\n  IV.3  THE LADDER (pratividhi)\n        VEDA-knowledge + SVADHARMA + ĀŚRAMA-discipline\n                    │\n            ┌───────┴───────┐\n          TAPAS  ──►  SATTVA  ──►  MANAS  ──►  ĀTMAN  ──►  NIVṚTTI\n          (austerity) (clarity)   (mind)     (Self)     (no return)\n\n  IV.4  THE MIND-DOCTRINE (15 ślokas)\n        ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n        │ fire-without-fuel → vṛtti-kṣaya (1)           │\n        │ ★ CITTAM EVA HI SAṂSĀRAḤ (3) ★               │\n        │ pure vs impure mind = kāma-saṅkalpa (6)       │\n        │ free of LAYA + VIKṢEPA → AMANĪ-BHĀVA (7)      │\n        │ water-in-water · fire-in-fire (10)            │\n        │ ★ MANA EVA … BANDHA-MOKṢAYOḤ (11) ★          │\n        └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                    │  then turns to ADORATION:\n        KAUTSĀYANI-STUTI (12–15): &quot;tvaṃ brahmā tvaṃ viṣṇuḥ …&quot;\n                    │\n  IV.5  THE COSMOS BEHIND IT\n        tamas ──(impelled by para)──► rajas ──► sattva\n                                              │\n                            conscious AṂŚA = kṣetrajña = PRAJĀPATI\n                            ┌──────────┬──────────┐\n                       BRAHMĀ      VIṢṆU       RUDRA\n                       (rajas)    (sattva)    (tamas)\n                            └──── one Self ────┘\n                       3 · 8 · 11 · 12 · ∞-fold  →  ANTAR BAHIŚ CA\n                                                    (within &amp; without)</pre>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka IV · Prapāṭhaka IV — Practical steps and observations","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka IV","title":"Practical steps and observations","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Run the simile-chain (IV.2).</strong> When an object grips you, name it with one of Maitri's fourteen similes — \"this is a painted wall,\" \"this is the plantain-pith,\" \"this is the conjuror's trick.\" The practice converts abstract <em>vairāgya</em> into a concrete, repeatable act of re-seeing: the <em>anartha</em> masquerading as <em>artha</em>.","2. <strong>Climb the ladder in order (IV.3).</strong> Maitri is emphatic that meditation fails for the <em>atapaska</em>. Do not skip to \"stilling the mind\" before the groundwork — <em>tapas</em> (disciplined simplicity) purifies toward <em>sattva</em>; only the <em>sāttvic</em> mind becomes still enough to reach the Self. Diagnose which rung you are actually on.","3. <strong>The redirection drill (IV.4.5).</strong> Verse 5 is a practice in itself: observe how <em>intensely</em> the mind clings to a desired object, then deliberately turn <em>that same intensity</em> toward Brahman / the Self. The verse promises that attachment, merely redirected, becomes the engine of release.","4. <strong>Subtract kāma-saṅkalpa (IV.4.6).</strong> Purifying the mind is defined not as adding anything but as <em>removing</em> desire-driven intention. In practice: catch the <em>saṅkalpa</em> (the \"I want / I'll get\") at its inception and let it lapse; what remains is the \"pure mind.\"","5. <strong>Guard against both laya and vikṣepa (IV.4.7).</strong> In sitting, watch for the two failure-modes by name: <em>laya</em> (the slide into drowsy blankness — counter with alertness/posture) and <em>vikṣepa</em> (the scatter into thought — counter with a return to the object). The \"supreme state\" lies between them, in <em>amanī-bhāva</em>.","6. <strong>\"What your mind is, that you become\" (IV.4.3).</strong> Treat this as a daily razor: the contents you habitually dwell on are literally shaping what you are becoming. Curating attention is therefore not optional self-improvement but the whole of the path — <em>guhyam etat sanātanam</em>.","7. <strong>Hold jñāna and bhakti together (IV.4.11–15).</strong> Maitri places the driest mind-analysis and the most fervent hymn side by side. Let analysis prevent sentimentality and let adoration prevent dryness; the <em>paraṃ padam</em> of the mind-verses and the \"<em>tvam</em>\" of the hymn are the same. The closing posture is <em>namaḥ śāntātmane</em> — homage to the tranquil Self that is also one's own."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Installment colophon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Thus the first four Prapāṭhakas of the Maitrāyaṇī Upaniṣad — the renunciation of Bṛhadratha (I), the inner Self and the fivefold breath (II), the elemental self bound by the guṇas (III), and the remedy of tapas, the purified mind, and the guṇa-cosmos (IV) — the philosophical core of the work.</em>","<strong>To be appended in a later session — Prapāṭhakas V–VII:</strong> the Kuṭsāyana hymn and the dual Brahman (V); the great supplement of thirty-eight sections (VI) — the two forms of Brahman, the <em>savitṛ</em>/Gāyatrī meditation, the <em>anna</em> (food) as Brahman, the <em>śabda-brahman → para-brahman</em> ascent (VI.22), the sixfold yoga (<em>prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhyāna, dhāraṇā, tarka, samādhi</em> — VI.18), the <em>nāḍī</em>-ascent and the inner <em>nāda</em> (VI.20–22), the sun-Puruṣa and the <em>amṛta-bindu</em> (VI.34–38); and the closing sections (VII) — the fires, the false teachers and the <em>avidyā</em>-polemic, ending <em>namo brahmaṇe</em>.","<em>(The closing apparatus — full Visual-Representations synthesis, consolidated Practical-Steps, Bibliography, and the corpus-wide Cross-references table — will be completed with that final installment, when the whole text is in hand.)</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
